\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_08_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_09_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_10_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_11_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_12_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_13_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_14_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_15_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_16_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_17_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_18_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\random_size_matched_19_genes.txt\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\replicate_support.csv\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\resolution_seed_metrics.csv\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\source_feature_to_symbol.csv\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\stability_metrics.csv\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\stratified_cell_type_associations.csv\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\summary.json\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\umap_coordinates.csv\nresults\\cd8_analysis\\within_replicate_sensitivity.csv\nreport\\main.tex\ninputs\\Cells.csv\ninputs\\Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx\ninputs\\Genes.txt\ninputs\\input-provenance.json\ninputs\\metabolic_genes_total.csv\ninputs\\Samples.csv\ninputs\\staging_manifest.json\nfigures\\clustering_diagnostics.pdf\nfigures\\clustering_diagnostics.png\nfigures\\clustering_diagnostics.svg\nfigures\\metabolic_umap.pdf\nfigures\\metabolic_umap.png\nfigures\\metabolic_umap.svg\nfigures\\cd8_analysis\\clustering_diagnostics.pdf\nfigures\\cd8_analysis\\clustering_diagnostics.png\nfigures\\cd8_analysis\\clustering_diagnostics.svg\nfigures\\cd8_analysis\\metabolic_umap.pdf\nfigures\\cd8_analysis\\metabolic_umap.png\nfigures\\cd8_analysis\\metabolic_umap.svg"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:6","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e53c9fb7c335ee0","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:21:18.810Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt, a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Transcriptional Metabolic States in Meningioma Brain Single-Cell Data}}\n\\author{Analysis of 3CA Study 3ca:20773 (Choudhury et al. 2022)}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes transcriptional metabolic states using user-supplied metabolic genes on single-cell RNA-seq data from meningioma brain tissue (3CA study 3ca:20773). We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells. Results are descriptive and exploratory; they do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data and Gene Set}\nWe analyzed 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples using the user-supplied metabolic gene set \\textit{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv} (SHA-256: \\texttt{7503180d29693bd1e654ea93281db0e3ec4177ff44842c8b02f6a098402e41a9}). The gene set contained 1,988 rows with 1,457 unique case-insensitive matches to expression symbols, 341 exact matches, and 190 unmatched symbols. Duplicate expression symbol rows were summed in raw counts before QC/normalization.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Clustering}\nCells were normalized by per-cell library size to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{normalized}_{ij} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI}_{ij}}{\\sum_j \\text{UMI}_{ij}} \\times 10,000\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $i$ indexes cells and $j$ indexes genes. Features were scaled using Scanpy's zero-centering with max value 10. PCA (30 components, arpack solver) was followed by k-nearest neighbors (15 neighbors) and Leiden clustering (igraph flavor, 2 iterations). Resolution was selected using median silhouette as the primary criterion with mean pairwise ARI as tie-breaker, filtering for stability via mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75.\n\n\\subsection{Controls and Metrics}\nWe compared the metabolic gene clustering against: (i) HVG-based clustering (highest variance of log1p-normalized expression among prevalence-passing non-mitochondrial symbols), and (ii) 19 size-matched random gene controls sampled without replacement. Metrics included silhouette score (estimated on 3,000 cells), Calinski-Harabasz index, Davies-Bouldin index, and pairwise ARI/NMI against cell type annotations.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Transcriptional Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes. Cells are colored by L\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 13689 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nlic states in meningioma brain single-cell data. The results are descriptive and exploratory; they do not establish discrete biological metabolic states or their absence.\n\n\\textbf{Question 1:} Metabolic genes induce cell-group separation across all cells, but the clustering is comparable to random controls and inferior to HVG-based clustering. The conclusion is \\textit{inconclusive} for establishing discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\textbf{Question 2:} Metabolic clusters show strong association with cell type annotations (NMI 0.496 for cell type, 0.648 for cell subtype), indicating that clustering is largely driven by cell-type composition rather than metabolic state. Confounder NMI values for patient (0.518) and sample (0.548) are substantial, suggesting clustering is dominated by sample/patient effects.\n\n\\textbf{Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells, metabolic genes induce 6 clusters, but the clustering is comparable to random controls and inferior to HVG. Confounder NMI values for patient (0.393) and sample (0.443) are substantial. The conclusion is \\textit{inconclusive} for establishing discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\textbf{Limitations:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette is estimated on a fixed random subset; all QC-passing cells are retained for PCA, graph construction, and clustering.\n    \\item The unsupervised question has no predefined condition contrast; no cell-level differential-expression P values are reported.\n    \\item No significance test is performed on data-derived cluster labels; within-replicate reclustering is descriptive and not held-out donor validation.\n    \\item Random gene controls are matched in size, not expression mean or dispersion; their exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\n    \\item A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\n    \\item Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\n    \\item Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section*{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, Briana C. Prager, William C. Chen, Martha A. Cady, Kyounghee Seo, Calixto-Hope G. Lucas, Tim J. Casey-Clyde, et al.\n``Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.''\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"task_checkpoint:2839e6f494c48cef76c60cf7c68695944916b09d06b8c484455eb2558a4ddea5","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83ca6674890abf08","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:21:31.369Z","result":"Task checkpoint saved. Continue the next concrete action; checkpoint claims are not completion evidence."},{"key":"list_files:76ceb187801cf64880338add683a7690e5e1c19f3ccb894800c8cee8290c22c0:6","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7e48b420a3431d6","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:21:34.113Z","result":"report\\main.tex"},{"key":"read_file:ace4fac137e0fc91f7c1d4b03c5efb8ae95e19f3484b849712cc918dce9edbaa:6","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a3476287ee635b23","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:21:07.273Z","result":"{\n  \"engine\": \"workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2\",\n  \"engine_path\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\Desktop\\\\agentic\\\\workflow_codex\\\\tools\\\\research-quality\\\\metabolic_states.py\",\n  \"engine_sha256\": \"51ddbf26c336a932f10d216db6092bc5aa24f402adf9dafee6a0435382592401\",\n  \"methods\": {\n    \"unit\": \"Exact-symbol aggregated raw counts; all source features form each cell library-size denominator\",\n    \"normalization\": \"Per-cell library size to 10000, then log1