 user-supplied metabolic genes on the Choudhury et al. 2022 brain single-cell RNA-seq dataset (3CA:20773).\n\n## Dataset\n\n- **Study**: Choudhury et al. 2022 - \"Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities\"\n- **3CA ID**: 3ca:20773\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8\n- **Technology**: 10x Genomics\n- **Samples**: 10\n- **Total cells**: 58,843\n\n## Data Sources\n\n### 3CA Raw Count Data\n- **Archive**: Data_Choudhury2022_Brain.tar.gz\n- **SHA-256**: 2156a73fd5f95a198ef90821f2d734bfa7e0b5aa3686deefd1b5c5d5d1269aa1\n- **Expression matrix**: inputs/Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx (33,538 genes × 58,843 cells)\n- **Cells metadata**: inputs/Cells.csv\n- **Genes list**: inputs/Genes.txt\n\n### User-Supplied Metabolic Genes\n- **File**: inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv\n- **SHA-256**: 7503180d29693bd1e654ea93281db0e3ec4177ff44842c8b02f6a098402e41a9\n- **Source gene count**: 1,988 symbols\n- **Matched genes after prevalence filtering**: 1,646 (global analysis), 1,318 (CD8 T cells subset)\n\n## Analysis Results\n\n### Question 1: Metabolic State Clustering (All Cells)\n\n**Analysis scope**: 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes\n\n**Clustering results**:\n- **Number of clusters**: 17\n- **Selected resolution**: 0.8 (highest median silhouette across stable candidates)\n- **Cluster size range**: 714 - 8,806 cells\n\n**Metrics**:\n- Silhouette: 0.217\n- Calinski-Harabasz: 6,731\n- Davies-Bouldin: 1.58\n- Mean pairwise ARI: 0.965\n- Mean pairwise NMI: 0.968\n\n**Control comparisons**:\n- HVG silhouette: 0.283 (stronger than metabolic features)\n- Random control silhouette range: 0.20 - 0.22\n- Metabolic-matched KMeans silhouette: 0.220\n- Random controls exceeding or equal: 2 of 19 (add-one rank fraction: 0.15)\n\n**Confounder analysis**:\n- Patient NMI: 0.518\n- Sample NMI: 0.548\n- Cell type NMI: 0.496\n- Cell subtype NMI: 0.648\n\n**Conclusion**: Inconclusive. The analysis describes cell-group separation using metabolic genes but does not establish discrete biological metabolic states. The HVG baseline shows stronger clustering than metabolic features. Random controls show similar silhouette values, and the random control exceedance fraction (0.15) is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n\n### Question 2: Cell Type Association\n\n**Global cell-type association**:\n- Cell type global NMI: 0.496\n- Within-replicate NMI range: 0.379 - 0.745 (median: 0.592)\n- Replicates assessable: 6\n\n**Cell subtype association**:\n- Cell subtype global NMI: 0.648\n- Within-replicate NMI range: 0.224 - 0.680 (median: 0.636)\n- Replicates assessable: 6\n\n**Within-replicate sensitivity**:\n- Replicates analyzed: 6\n- Global vs within ARI range: 0.071 - 0.676\n- Weakest replicate: 3 (3,287 cells)\n\n**Interpretation**: Cell type and cell subtype show moderate association with metabolic clusters (NMI ~0.5-0.65). The within-replicate \n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 8052 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nity**: The within-replicate NMI varies substantially, with some replicates showing weaker associations. This limits the ability to claim robust metabolic state separation.\n\n4. **Random control comparison**: The random control exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value. In the global analysis, 2 of 19 random controls exceeded the metabolic features (0.15 add-one rank fraction). In the CD8 analysis, 17 of 19 exceeded (0.9 add-one rank fraction).\n\n## Limitations\n\n- **Descriptive only**: All metrics are descriptive; no significance tests are performed on data-derived cluster labels.\n- **No independent validation**: Within-replicate reclustering is not held-out donor validation.\n- **Confounder sensitivity**: Patient and sample NMI values indicate potential confounding by these factors.\n- **Continuum vs discrete**: The analysis does not test whether metabolic states are discrete or continuous.\n- **Symbol-level aggregation**: Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization, which preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs.\n\n## Files\n\n- `report/main.tex` - LaTeX report\n- `report/main.pdf` - Compiled PDF report\n- `results/summary.json` - Global analysis summary\n- `results/analysis_manifest.json` - Analysis manifest with all metadata\n- `results/core_analysis/core_result.json` - Global core analysis results\n- `results/core_analysis/cluster_labels.csv` - Global cluster labels\n- `results/core_analysis/matched_metabolic_genes.txt` - Matched metabolic genes\n- `results/core_analysis/baseline_metrics.csv` - HVG and random control metrics\n- `results/core_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf` - Global metabolic UMAP\n- `results/core_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf` - Global clustering diagnostics\n- `results/cd8_analysis/core_result.json` - CD8 T cell core analysis results\n- `results/cd8_analysis/cluster_labels.csv` - CD8 T cell cluster labels\n- `results/cd8_analysis/matched_metabolic_genes.txt` - Matched metabolic genes for CD8\n- `results/cd8_analysis/baseline_metrics.csv` - CD8 T cell baseline metrics\n- `results/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf` - CD8 T cell metabolic UMAP\n- `results/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf` - CD8 T cell clustering diagnostics\n- `inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv` - User-supplied metabolic genes\n- `inputs/Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx` - Expression matrix\n- `inputs/Cells.csv` - Cell metadata\n- `inputs/Genes.txt` - Gene list\n\n## References\n\n1. Choudhury, A., Magill, S. T., Eaton, C. D., et al. (2022). Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities. *Nature Genetics*, 54(5), 649-659. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8\n\n## Verification\n\nThe analysis was performed using the `workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2` engine. Core result hashes:\n- Global core_result.json: 0da19b546073463f6341cb411b21287f3e50b2b9733a6f39dfc53e1fb9ca5848\n- CD8 core_result.json: 50950f343600c9c0dd17ed5191a3511cc2fb397d1b026120f4fe47ca6ab1f102\n"},{"key":"task_checkpoint:09b8624dc9a0374eebe082082d982b2425d602036281eb33402940c2a97ae69f","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a230a321ff3709f4","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:55:05.088Z","result":"Task checkpoint saved. Continue the next concrete action; checkpoint claims are not completion evidence."},{"key":"replace_in_file:43def6ecf4e23d9bd41904f2931cfe1aa23df23b5f3b3520bede3f955f97257b","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be77feb30e1c192","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:53:40.067Z","result":"Replaced one block in report\\main.tex"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:17","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5639443415ae5c0","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:55:38.404Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt, a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Transcriptional Metabolic States in Meningioma Brain Single-Cell Data}}\n\\author{Analysis of 3CA Study 3ca:20773 (Choudhury et al. 2022)}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes transcriptional metabolic states using user-supplied metabolic genes on single-cell RNA-seq data from meningioma brain tissue (3CA study 3ca:20773). We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells. Results are descriptive and exploratory; they do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data and Gene Set}\nWe analyzed 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples using the user-supplied metabolic gene set \\textit{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv}. The gene set contained 1,988 rows with 1,457 unique case-insensitive matches to expression symbols, 341 exact matches, and 190 unmatched symbols. Duplicate expression symbol rows were summed in raw counts before QC/normalization.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Clustering}\nCells were normalized by per-cell library size to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{normalized}_{ij} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI}_{ij}}{\\sum_j \\text{UMI}_{ij}} \\times 10,000\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $i$ indexes cells and $j$ indexes genes. Features were scaled using Scanpy's zero-centering with max value 10. PCA (30 components, arpack solver) was followed by k-nearest neighbors (15 neighbors) and Leiden clustering (igraph flavor, 2 iterations). Resolution was selected using median silhouette as the primary criterion with mean pairwise ARI as tie-breaker, filtering for stability via mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75.\n\n\\subsection{Controls and Metrics}\nWe compared the metabolic gene clustering against: (i) HVG-based clustering (highest variance of log1p-normalized expression among prevalence-passing non-mitochondrial symbols), and (ii) 19 size-matched random gene controls.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Transcriptional Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\nThe global analysis of 58,843 cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes produced 17 Leiden clusters at resolution 0.8. The clustering was selected based on highest median silhouette (0.222) across seeds, with mean pairwise ARI (0.965) as tie-breaker. Stability filtering required mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75, which was satisfied by resolution 0.8. The silhouette score was 0.217, Calinski-Harabasz was 6,731, and Davies-Bouldin was 1.58. Mean pairwise ARI against cell type ann\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 12000 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nouette 0.168.}\n    \\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Random control comparison for CD8 T cell metabolic gene clustering (3,624 cells, 1,318 genes).}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_random_controls}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Method & Silhouette & Random controls exceeding or equal \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Metabolic genes & 0.099 & 17 of 19 \\\\\n        HVG-based & 0.168 & -- \\\\\n        Random controls (mean) & 0.107 & -- \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Confounder NMI values for CD8 T cell analysis (3,624 cells).}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_confounder_nmi}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Confounder & NMI \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Patient & 0.393 \\\\\n        Sample & 0.443 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe within-replicate summary shows 3 replicates were analyzed, with global-vs-within ARI ranging from 0.511 to 0.845, with replicate 6 being the weakest (2,776 cells).\n\nThe conclusion is \\textit{inconclusive} for establishing discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette is estimated on a fixed random subset; all QC-passing cells are retained for PCA, graph construction, and clustering.\n    \\item The unsupervised question has no predefined condition contrast; no cell-level differential-expression P values are reported.\n    \\item No significance test is performed on data-derived cluster labels; within-replicate reclustering is descriptive and not held-out donor validation.\n    \\item Random gene controls are matched in size, not expression mean or dispersion; their exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\n    \\item A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\n    \\item Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\n    \\item Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section*{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, Briana C. Prager, William C. Chen, Martha A. Cady, Kyounghee Seo, Calixto-Hope G. Lucas, Tim J. Casey-Clyde, et al.\n``Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.''\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"write_file:9a16539486978e1c55f6d404538172781f51ce897e638e02ebae2285e4206c26","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bb3b92dab33e4b50","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:55:44.570Z","result":"ERROR: Path leaves workspace: C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\3CA_R4\\figures\\check_files.txt"},{"key":"read_file:c63d0be4b238f04cf533c9557adcf6ebf1d2a7ec74228ba937e760e2f7d5e56b:17","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-913403b51b55e213","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:54:50.147Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt, a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Transcriptional Metabolic States in Meningioma Brain Single-Cell Data}}\n\\author{Analysis of 3CA Study 3ca:20773 (Choudhury et al. 2022)}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes transcriptional metabolic states using user-supplied metabolic genes on single-cell RNA-seq data from meningioma brain tissue (3CA study 3ca:20773). We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells. Results are descriptive and exploratory; they do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data and Gene Set}\nWe analyzed 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples using the user-supplied metabolic gene set \\textit{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv}. The gene set contained 1,988 rows with 1,457 unique case-insensitive matches to expression symbols, 341 exact matches, and 190 unmatched symbols. Duplicate expression symbol rows were summed in raw counts before QC/normalization.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Clustering}\nCells were normalized by per-cell library size to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{normalized}_{ij} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI}_{ij}}{\\sum_j \\text{UMI}_{ij}} \\times 10,000\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $i$ indexes cells and $j$ indexes genes. Features were scaled using Scanpy's zero-centering with max value 10. PCA (30 components, arpack solver) was followed by k-nearest neighbors (15 neighbors) and Leiden clustering (igraph flavor, 2 iterations). Resolution was selected using median silhouette as the primary criterion with mean pairwise ARI as tie-breaker, filtering for stability via mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75.\n\n\\subsection{Controls and Metrics}\nWe compared the metabolic gene clustering against: (i) HVG-based clustering (highest variance of log1p-normalized expression among prevalence-passing non-mitochondrial symbols), and (ii) 19 size-matched random gene controls.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Transcriptional Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\nThe global analysis of 58,843 cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes produced 17 Leiden clusters at resolution 0.8. The clustering was selected based on highest median silhouette (0.222) across seeds, with mean pairwise ARI (0.965) as tie-breaker. Stability filtering required mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75, which was satisfied by resolution 0.8. The silhouette score was 0.217, Calinski-Harabasz was 6,731, and Davies-Bouldin was 1.58. Mean pairwise ARI against cell type ann\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 12000 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nouette 0.168.}\n    \\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Random control comparison for CD8 T cell metabolic gene clustering (3,624 cells, 1,318 genes).}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_random_controls}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Method & Silhouette & Random controls exceeding or equal \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Metabolic genes & 0.099 & 17 of 19 \\\\\n        HVG-based & 0.168 & -- \\\\\n        Random controls (mean) & 0.107 & -- \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Confounder NMI values for CD8 T cell analysis (3,624 cells).}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_confounder_nmi}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Confounder & NMI \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Patient & 0.393 \\\\\n        Sample & 0.443 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe within-replicate summary shows 3 replicates were analyzed, with global-vs-within ARI ranging from 0.511 to 0.845, with replicate 6 being the weakest (2,776 cells).\n\nThe conclusion is \\textit{inconclusive} for establishing discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette is estimated on a fixed random subset; all QC-passing cells are retained for PCA, graph construction, and clustering.\n    \\item The unsupervised question has no predefined condition contrast; no cell-level differential-expression P values are reported.\n    \\item No significance test is performed on data-derived cluster labels; within-replicate reclustering is descriptive and not held-out donor validation.\n    \\item Random gene controls are matched in size, not expression mean or dispersion; their exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\n    \\item A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\n    \\item Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\n    \\item Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section*{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, Briana C. Prager, William C. Chen, Martha A. Cady, Kyounghee Seo, Calixto-Hope G. Lucas, Tim J. Casey-Clyde, et al.\n``Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.''\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:b332dd3ac635f078964f74933d499a662ef977b14f163e5c0cee10cab5c5b92c:17","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-80e122abcc0be3d9","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:56:02.849Z","result":"\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Transcriptional Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\nThe global analysis of 58,843 cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes produced 17 Leiden clusters at resolution 0.8. The clustering was selected based on highest median silhouette (0.222) across seeds, with mean pairwise ARI (0.965) as tie-breaker. Stability filtering required mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75, which was satisfied by resolution 0.8. The silhouette score was 0.217, Calinski-Harabasz was 6,731, and Davies-Bouldin was 1.58. Mean pairwise ARI against cell type annotations was 0.965, and mean pairwise NMI was 0.968. The metabolic gene clustering silhouette (0.217) was comparable to random controls (mean 0.210) and inferior to HVG-based clustering (0.283). The random control comparison showed 2 of 19 controls exceeded or equaled the metabolic clustering silhouette, yielding an add-one rank fraction of 0.15. This is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes. Cells are colored by 17 Leiden clusters at resolution 0.8. Cluster sizes range from 714 to 8,806 cells. The clustering was selected based on highest median silhouette (0.222) across seeds, with mean pairwise ARI (0.965) as tie-breaker. Stability filtering required mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75, which was satisfied by resolution 0.8. The silhouette score was 0.217, Calinski-Harabasz was 6,731, and Davies-Bouldin was 1.58. Mean pairwise ARI against cell type annotations was 0.965, and mean pairwise NMI was 0.968. The metabolic gene clustering silhouette (0.217) was comparable to random controls (mean 0.210) and inferior to HVG-based clustering (0.283). The random control comparison showed 2 of 19 controls exceeded or equaled the metabolic clustering silhouette, yielding an add-one rank fraction of 0.15. This is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.}\n    \\label{fig:metabolic_umap}\n\\end{figure}"},{"key":"read_file:3065bf4cf82d86503134b8d7b965b6a2430284840feb46eabcfe54e5c5c78461:17","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aafcbe92f01538cb","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T15:53:54.436Z","result":"%PDF-1.4\n%�� ��\n1 0 obj\n<< /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R >>\nendobj\n8 0 obj\n<< /Font 3 0 R /XObject 7 0 R /ExtGState 4 0 R /Pattern 5 0 R\n/Shading 6 0 R /ProcSet [ /PDF /Text /ImageB /ImageC /ImageI ] >>\nendobj\n11 0 obj\n<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /Resources 8 0 R\n/MediaBox [ 0 0 525.093625 294.068 ] /Contents 9 0 R /Annots 10 0 R >>\nendobj\n9 0 obj\n<< /Length 12 0 R /Filter /FlateDecode >>\nstream\nx��][�\u001c7�������{��N��e���QE\u0015\u0010s\u0005\u0014\u0005�8�b\u0002I>R���fwՏ��}gm��\u0005!/���i��Q��=Z?����|����v����3����Ş��?�O��\u00