resolution\": 0.8\n    }"},{"key":"read_file:f97b73c9adbef27612881199f539d29b09c5c08d9d569f60a0f43a299ae9b508:20","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-99fda81ea698a0ba","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T16:04:04.084Z","result":"\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Transcriptional Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 meningioma cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes. Each point represents a cell colored by Leiden cluster (17 clusters at resolution 0.8). Panel supports that metabolic genes induce clustering, but the low silhouette score (0.217) and high random control exceedance (17/19) indicate this does not establish discrete biological metabolic states.}\n    \\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n    \\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global analysis (58,843 cells, 1,646 genes). Panel shows silhouette scores across seeds and resolutions, with the selected resolution 0.8 marked. The median silhouette (0.222) and mean pairwise ARI (0.965) are reported in the text.}\n    \\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe global analysis of 58,843 cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes produced 17 Leiden clusters at resolution 0.8. The clustering was selected based on highest median silhouette (0.222) across seeds, with mean pairwise ARI (0.965) as tie-breaker. Stability filtering required mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75, which was satisfied by resolution 0.8. The silhouette score was 0.217, Calinski-Harabasz was 6,731, and Davies-Bouldin was 1.58. Mean pairwise ARI against cell type annotations was 0.965, and mean pairwise NMI was 0.968. The metabolic gene clustering silhouette (0.217) was comparable to random controls (mean 0.210) and inferior to HVG-based clustering (0.283). The random control comparison showed 2 of 19 controls exceeded or equaled the metabolic clustering silhouette, yielding an add-one rank fraction of 0.15. This is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n    \\centering"},{"key":"read_file:e1d112006a4a726f80691b10ea60033fc27313219f3522680c67a4cb69df93c5:20","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b55ee6fed6d763d7","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T16:04:10.999Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt, a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Transcriptional Metabolic States in Meningioma Brain Single-Cell Data}}\n\\author{Analysis of 3CA Study 3ca:20773 (Choudhury et al. 2022)}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes transcriptional metabolic states using user-supplied metabolic genes on single-cell RNA-seq data from meningioma brain tissue (3CA study 3ca:20773). We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells. Results are descriptive and exploratory; they do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data and Gene Set}\nWe analyzed 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples using the user-supplied metabolic gene set \\textit{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv}. The gene set contained 1,988 rows with 1,457 unique case-insensitive matches to expression symbols, 341 exact matches, and 190 unmatched symbols. Duplicate expression symbol rows were summed in raw counts before QC/normalization.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Clustering}\nCells were normalized by per-cell library size to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{normalized}_{ij} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI}_{ij}}{\\sum_j \\text{UMI}_{ij}} \\times 10,000\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $i$ indexes cells and $j$ indexes genes. Features were scaled using Scanpy's zero-centering with max value 10. PCA (30 components, arpack solver) was followed by k-nearest neighbors (15 neighbors) and Leiden clustering (igraph flavor, 2 iterations). Resolution was selected using median silhouette as the primary criterion with mean pairwise ARI as tie-breaker, filtering for stability via mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75.\n\n\\subsection{Controls and Metrics}\nWe compared the metabolic gene clustering against: (i) HVG-based clustering (highest variance of log1p-normalized expression among prevalence-passing non-mitochondrial symbols), and (ii) 19 size-matched random gene controls.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Transcriptional Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 meningioma cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes. Each point represents a cell colored by Leiden cluster (17 clusters at resolution 0.8). Panel supports that metabolic genes induce clustering, but the low silhouette score (0.217) and high random control exceedance (17/19) indicate this does not establish discrete biological metabolic\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 8125 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\narable to random controls (mean 0.210) and inferior to HVG-based clustering (0.283). The random control comparison showed 2 of 19 controls exceeded or equaled the metabolic clustering silhouette, yielding an add-one rank fraction of 0.15. This is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.}\n    \\label{fig:metabolic_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_diagnostics.pdf}\n    \\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global metabolic gene analysis (58,843 cells, 1,646 genes). Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across seeds; panel (b) shows cluster sizes. The resolution 0.8 was selected based on highest median silhouette (0.222) across seeds, with mean pairwise ARI (0.965) as tie-breaker. Stability filtering required mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75. The silhouette score was 0.217, Calinski-Harabasz was 6,731, and Davies-Bouldin was 1.58. Mean pairwise ARI against cell type annotations was 0.965, and mean pairwise NMI was 0.968. The metabolic gene clustering silhouette (0.217) was comparable to random controls (mean 0.210) and inferior to HVG-based clustering (0.283). The random control comparison showed 2 of 19 controls exceeded or equaled the metabolic clustering silhouette, yielding an add-one rank fraction of 0.15. This is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.}\n    \\label{fig:metabolic_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Question 2: Relationship Between Metabolic Groupings and Known Cell Types/Subtypes}\n\nThe global clustering shows strong association with cell type annotations (NMI = 0.496) and cell subtype annotations (NMI = 0.648). The cell type association summary indicates 6 replicates were assessable, with within-replicate NMI ranging from 0.379 to 0.745 (median = 0.592). The within-replicate summary shows 6 patients were analyzed, with global-vs-within ARI ranging from 0.071 to 0.676, with patient 3 being the weakest replicate (3,287 cells).\n\nThe confounder NMI values show patient (0.518) and sample (0.548) have moderate associations with clusters, suggesting clusters are partially driven by patient/sample structure. The cell type association (NMI = 0.496) indicates clusters are substantially aligned with cell type annotations rather than being independent biological states.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Random control comparison for global metabolic gene clustering (58,843 cells, 1,646 genes).}\n    \\label{tab:global_random_controls}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Method & Silhouette & Random controls exceeding or equal \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Metabolic genes & 0.217 & 2 of 19 \\\\\n        HVG-based & 0.283 & -- \\\\\n        Random controls (mean) & 0.210 & -- \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Confounder NMI values for global analysis (58,843 cells).}\n    \\label{tab:global_confounder_nmi}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Confounder & NMI \\\\"},{"key":"read_file:f3af0ec468c8400b3a4e2949f27ca18bebc36748a7cc7c2ffbdec49b1174278b:20","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5340c2dabdf5580","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T16:04:17.223Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt, a4paper]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Transcriptional Metabolic States in Meningioma Brain Single-Cell Data}}\n\\author{Analysis of 3CA Study 3ca:20773 (Choudhury et al. 2022)}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes transcriptional metabolic states using user-supplied metabolic genes on single-cell RNA-seq data from meningioma brain tissue (3CA study 3ca:20773). We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells. Results are descriptive and exploratory; they do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data and Gene Set}\nWe analyzed 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples using the user-supplied metabolic gene set \\textit{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv}. The gene set contained 1,988 rows with 1,457 unique case-insensitive matches to expression symbols, 341 exact matches, and 190 unmatched symbols. Duplicate expression symbol rows were summed in raw counts before QC/normalization.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Clustering}\nCells were normalized by per-cell library size to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{normalized}_{ij} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI}_{ij}}{\\sum_j \\text{UMI}_{ij}} \\times 10,000\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $i$ indexes cells and $j$ indexes genes. Features were scaled using Scanpy's zero-centering with max value 10. PCA (30 components, arpack solver) was followed by k-nearest neighbors (15 neighbors) and Leiden clustering (igraph flavor, 2 iterations). Resolution was selected using median silhouette as the primary criterion with mean pairwise ARI as tie-breaker, filtering for stability via mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75.\n\n\\subsection{Controls and Metrics}\nWe compared the metabolic gene clustering against: (i) HVG-based clustering (highest variance of log1p-normalized expression among prevalence-passing non-mitochondrial symbols), and (ii) 19 size-matched random gene controls.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Transcriptional Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 meningioma cells using 1,646 matched metabolic genes. Each point represents a cell colored by Leiden cluster (17 clusters at resolution 0.8). Panel supports that metabolic genes induce clustering, but the low silhouette score (0.217) and high random control exceedance (17/19) indicate this does not establish discrete biological metabolic\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 14864 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nconclusion is \\textit{inconclusive} for establishing discrete biological metabolic states. The low silhouette scores (0.217 global, 0.099 CD8) and high random control exceedance suggest that metabolic gene clustering does not reveal robust, reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings beyond what is expected by chance.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Random control comparison for CD8 T cell metabolic gene clustering (3,624 cells, 1,318 genes).}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_random_controls}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Method & Silhouette & Random controls exceeding or equal \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Metabolic genes & 0.099 & 17 of 19 \\\\\n        HVG-based & 0.168 & -- \\\\\n        Random controls (mean) & 0.107 & -- \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Confounder NMI values for CD8 T cell analysis (3,624 cells).}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_confounder_nmi}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Confounder & NMI \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Patient & 0.393 \\\\\n        Sample & 0.443 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette is estimated on a fixed random subset; all QC-passing cells are retained for PCA, graph construction, and clustering.\n    \\item The unsupervised question has no predefined condition contrast; no cell-level differential-expression P values are reported.\n    \\item No significance test is performed on data-derived cluster labels; within-replicate reclustering is descriptive and not held-out donor validation.\n    \\item Random gene controls are matched in size, not expression mean or dispersion; their exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\n    \\item A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\n    \\item Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\n    \\item Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section*{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, Briana C. Prager, William C. Chen, Martha A. Cady, Kyounghee Seo, Calixto-Hope G. Lucas, Tim J. Casey-Clyde, et al.\n``Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.''\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:20","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c1bdaeb36054295","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T16:02:53.453Z","result