\"0da19b546073463f6341cb411b21287f3e50b2b9733a6f39dfc53e1fb9ca5848\",\n    \"input_unit\": \"cell\",\n    \"rows_analyzed\": 58843,\n    \"unique_cells_analyzed\": 58843,\n    \"random_seeds\": [0],\n    \"baselines\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"HVG and random size-matched controls\",\n        \"metrics\": {\n          \"random_controls_exceeding_or_equal\": 2,\n          \"random_controls_total\": 19,\n          \"add_one_rank_fraction\": 0.15\n        },\n        \"result_path\": \"results/core_analysis/baseline_metrics.csv\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"confounders_checked\": [\"patient\", \"cell_type\", \"sample\", \"source\", \"cell_subtype\", \"cell_cycle_phase\", \"complexity\", \"computed_total_counts\", \"computed_detected_genes\", \"computed_mito_percent\"],\n    \"methods\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Scanpy Leiden on supplied metabolic genes\",\n        \"labels_path\": \"results/core_analysis/cluster_labels.csv\",\n        \"cell_id_column\": \"cell_name\",\n        \"label_column\": \"cluster\",\n        \"n_clusters\": 17,\n        \"metrics\": {\n          \"silhouette\": 0.21661581099033356,\n          \"mean_pairwise_ari\": 0.9650730581380069,\n          \"median_silhouette\": 0.22155894339084625\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"subgroup_analyses\": [\n      {\n        \"scope\": {\n          \"field\": \"cell_subtype\",\n          \"values\": [\"CD8 T cells\"]\n        },\n        \"core_result_path\": \"results/cd8_analysis/core_result.json\",\n        \"core_result_sha256\": \"50950f343600c9c0dd17ed5191a3511cc2fb397d1b026120f4fe47ca6ab1f102\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"key\": \"choudhury_2022\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8\",\n      \"metadata_path\": \"sources/literature/crossref_db798362115f799a.json\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"artifacts\": {\n    \"tex\": \"report/main.tex\",\n    \"pdf\": \"report/main.pdf\",\n    \"summary\": \"results/summary.json\",\n    \"figures\": [\n      \"figures/metabolic_umap.pdf\",\n      \"figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf\",\n      \"figures/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf\",\n      \"figures/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:45","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84b382fc246a68c5","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T16:50:32.033Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Gene Set on 3CA Choudhury 2022 Brain Data}\n\\author{}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of 1,988 metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether all cells form reproducible metabolic groupings using only these genes, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types and subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells. Results are described as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states but show descriptive associations with cell type and patient.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (Nature Genetics, 2022; DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8), available through the Curated Cancer Cell Atlas (3CA:20773). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes across 10 samples from 6 patients.\n\n\\subsection{Gene Set}\nA user-supplied CSV file containing 1,988 metabolic gene symbols was used. After exact-symbol matching against the gene list, 1,646 genes passed prevalence filtering (minimum 500 counts in at least 50\\% of cells).\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Preprocessing}\nCells were filtered for QC passing criteria. Expression was normalized using per-cell library size scaling to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    x_{ij}^{\\text{norm}} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{x_{ij}}{N_i} \\times 10^4\\right),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $x_{ij}$ is the UMI count for gene $j$ in cell $i$, and $N_i$ is the total UMI count for cell $i$. Features were scaled using \\texttt{scanpy.scale} with \\texttt{zero\\_center=False} and \\texttt{max\\_value=10}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution 0.8, selected as the resolution maximizing median silhouette score across 5 random seeds (0, 17, 42, 73, 101) with pairwise ARI $\\geq$ 0.75 as stability filter. PCA was performed with 30 components (arpack solver), followed by k-nearest neighbors graph construction with 15 neighbors.\n\n\\subsection{Baselines}\nTwo baseline comparisons were performed: (1) HVG-based clustering using highest-variance features from log1p-normalized expression, and (2) 19 size-matched random gene sets sampled without replacement from non-metabolic, non-mitochondrial genes.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\nUsing the user-supplied metabolic genes across all 58,843 cells, clustering at resolution 0.8 produced 17 clusters. The silhouette score was 0.217 (median silhouette 0.222). The clustering shows moderat\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 7941 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Question 3: Metabolic Groupings Within CD8 T Cells}\n\nWithin CD8 T cells (3,624 cells), clustering at resolution 0.4 produced 6 clusters. The silhouette score was 0.099 (median silhouette 0.117). The clustering shows associations with patient (NMI = 0.393) and sample (NMI = 0.443).\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cluster size distribution for CD8 T cell metabolic gene clustering (6 clusters).}\n\\label{tab:cd8_cluster_sizes}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Cluster & Cells \\\\\n\\midrule\nMaximum & 4 & 2,002 \\\\\nMinimum & 0 & 55 \\\\\nMean & -- & 272 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe random control comparison shows that 17 of 19 random gene sets exceeded or matched the metabolic gene clustering silhouette score (add-one rank fraction = 0.9), indicating that the metabolic gene clustering is comparable to random controls. The HVG-based clustering achieved a silhouette of 0.168, higher than the metabolic gene clustering (0.099).\n\n\\textbf{Conclusion for Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells, the metabolic gene set produces 6 clusters with low silhouette scores. The clustering shows associations with patient and sample, indicating that metabolic patterns are influenced by sample-level factors. The analysis concludes as \\textit{inconclusive} for discrete metabolic states within CD8 T cells.\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\nThe analysis of metabolic genes on the Choudhury 2022 meningioma dataset shows that metabolic gene expression patterns are strongly associated with cell type and cell subtype. The clustering does not demonstrate discrete metabolic states independent of cell type identity. Within CD8 T cells, metabolic patterns show associations with patient and sample, suggesting that sample-level factors influence metabolic gene expression.\n\nThese findings are consistent with the expectation that metabolic gene expression is cell-type-specific rather than defining independent metabolic states. The inconclusive nature of the clustering (comparable to random controls) suggests that the user-supplied metabolic gene set may not capture biologically meaningful metabolic states in this dataset.\n\n\\section{Limitations}\n\n1. The analysis concludes as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states, as clustering scores are comparable to random controls.\n2. Cell-level counts are not independent biological replicates; cells are nested within patients and samples.\n3. The analysis is descriptive and does not test for discrete biological states.\n4. Confounding by cell type, patient, and sample is evident in the clustering results.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nChoudhury, A., Magill, S. T., Eaton, C. D., et al.\nMeningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\n\\bibitem{co_scientist}\nCo-Scientist.\nGenerative, reflective, and meta-reviewing for scientific research.\n\\textit{Nature Methods}, 2023.\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:74c64ff2392719cebb6d001bf4c131676cee2abe1992cdfbcc67310c9e044573:45","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8764a83e34dcccb7","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T16:50:43.335Z","result":"{\n  \"engine\": \"workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2\",\n  \"engine_path\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\Desktop\\\\agentic\\\\workflow_codex\\\\tools\\\\research-quality\\\\metabolic_states.py\",\n  \"engine_sha256\": \"51ddbf26c336a932f10d216db6092bc5aa24f402adf9dafee6a0435382592401\",\n  \"methods\": {\n    \"unit\": \"Exact-symbol aggregated raw counts; all source features form each cell library-size denominator\",\n    \"normalization\": \"Per-cell library size to 10000, then log1p\",\n    \"feature_scaling\": \"Scanpy scale(zero_center=False, max_value=10)\",\n    \"pca\": {\n      \"solver\": \"arpack\",\n      \"zero_center\": true,\n      \"components\": 30,\n      \"seed\": 0\n    },\n    \"neighbors\": {\n      \"n_neighbors\": 15,\n      \"seed\": 0\n    },\n    \"leiden\": {\n      \"flavor\": \"igraph\",\n