agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:21:31.358Z","result":"# QA_R4: Metabolic State Analysis of Meningioma Single-Cell Data\n\n## Overview\n\nThis repository contains the analysis of transcriptional metabolic states using user-supplied metabolic genes on the Choudhury et al. 2022 brain single-cell RNA-seq dataset (3CA:20773).\n\n## Dataset\n\n- **Study**: Choudhury et al. 2022 - \"Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities\"\n- **3CA ID**: 3ca:20773\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8\n- **Technology**: 10x Genomics\n- **Samples**: 10\n- **Total cells**: 58,843\n\n## Data Sources\n\n### 3CA Raw Count Data\n- **Archive**: Data_Choudhury2022_Brain.tar.gz\n- **SHA-256**: 2156a73fd5f95a198ef90821f2d734bfa7e0b5aa3686deefd1b5c5d5d1269aa1\n- **Expression matrix**: inputs/Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx (33,538 genes × 58,843 cells)\n- **Cells metadata**: inputs/Cells.csv\n- **Genes list**: inputs/Genes.txt\n\n### User-Supplied Metabolic Genes\n- **File**: inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv\n- **SHA-256**: 7503180d29693bd1e654ea93281db0e3ec4177ff44842c8b02f6a098402e41a9\n- **Source gene count**: 1,988 symbols\n- **Matched genes after prevalence filtering**: 1,646 (global analysis), 1,318 (CD8 T cells subset)\n\n## Analysis Results\n\n### Question 1: Metabolic State Clustering (All Cells)\n\n**Analysis scope**: 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes\n\n**Clustering results**:\n- **Number of clusters**: 17\n- **Selected resolution**: 0.8 (highest median silhouette across stable candidates)\n- **Cluster size range**: 714 - 8,806 cells\n\n**Metrics**:\n- Silhouette: 0.217\n- Calinski-Harabasz: 6,731\n- Davies-Bouldin: 1.58\n- Mean pairwise ARI: 0.965\n- Mean pairwise NMI: 0.968\n\n**Control comparisons**:\n- HVG silhouette: 0.283 (stronger than metabolic features)\n- Random control silhouette range: 0.20 - 0.22\n- Metabolic-matched KMeans silhouette: 0.220\n- Random controls exceeding or equal: 2 of 19 (add-one rank fraction: 0.15)\n\n**Confounder analysis**:\n- Patient NMI: 0.518\n- Sample NMI: 0.548\n- Cell type NMI: 0.496\n- Cell subtype NMI: 0.648\n\n**Conclusion**: Inconclusive. The analysis describes cell-group separation using metabolic genes but does not establish discrete biological metabolic states. The HVG baseline shows stronger clustering than metabolic features. Random controls show similar silhouette values, and the random control exceedance fraction (0.15) is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n\n### Question 2: Cell Type Association\n\n**Global cell-type association**:\n- Cell type global NMI: 0.496\n- Within-replicate NMI range: 0.379 - 0.745 (median: 0.592)\n- Replicates assessable: 6\n\n**Cell subtype association**:\n- Cell subtype global NMI: 0.648\n- Within-replicate NMI range: 0.224 - 0.680 (median: 0.636)\n- Replicates assessable: 6\n\n**Within-replicate sensitivity**:\n- Replicates analyzed: 6\n- Global vs within ARI range: 0.071 - 0.676\n- Weakest replicate: 3 (3,287 cells)\n\n**Interpretation**: Cell type and cell subtype show moderate association with metabolic clusters (NMI ~0.5-0.65). The within-replicate \n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 8052 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nity**: The within-replicate NMI varies substantially, with some replicates showing weaker associations. This limits the ability to claim robust metabolic state separation.\n\n4. **Random control comparison**: The random control exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value. In the global analysis, 2 of 19 random controls exceeded the metabolic features (0.15 add-one rank fraction). In the CD8 analysis, 17 of 19 exceeded (0.9 add-one rank fraction).\n\n## Limitations\n\n- **Descriptive only**: All metrics are descriptive; no significance tests are performed on data-derived cluster labels.\n- **No independent validation**: Within-replicate reclustering is not held-out donor validation.\n- **Confounder sensitivity**: Patient and sample NMI values indicate potential confounding by these factors.\n- **Continuum vs discrete**: The analysis does not test whether metabolic states are discrete or continuous.\n- **Symbol-level aggregation**: Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization, which preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs.\n\n## Files\n\n- `report/main.tex` - LaTeX report\n- `report/main.pdf` - Compiled PDF report\n- `results/summary.json` - Global analysis summary\n- `results/analysis_manifest.json` - Analysis manifest with all metadata\n- `results/core_analysis/core_result.json` - Global core analysis results\n- `results/core_analysis/cluster_labels.csv` - Global cluster labels\n- `results/core_analysis/matched_metabolic_genes.txt` - Matched metabolic genes\n- `results/core_analysis/baseline_metrics.csv` - HVG and random control metrics\n- `results/core_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf` - Global metabolic UMAP\n- `results/core_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf` - Global clustering diagnostics\n- `results/cd8_analysis/core_result.json` - CD8 T cell core analysis results\n- `results/cd8_analysis/cluster_labels.csv` - CD8 T cell cluster labels\n- `results/cd8_analysis/matched_metabolic_genes.txt` - Matched metabolic genes for CD8\n- `results/cd8_analysis/baseline_metrics.csv` - CD8 T cell baseline metrics\n- `results/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf` - CD8 T cell metabolic UMAP\n- `results/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf` - CD8 T cell clustering diagnostics\n- `inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv` - User-supplied metabolic genes\n- `inputs/Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx` - Expression matrix\n- `inputs/Cells.csv` - Cell metadata\n- `inputs/Genes.txt` - Gene list\n\n## References\n\n1. Choudhury, A., Magill, S. T., Eaton, C. D., et al. (2022). Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities. *Nature Genetics*, 54(5), 649-659. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8\n\n## Verification\n\nThe analysis was performed using the `workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2` engine. Core result hashes:\n- Global core_result.json: 0da19b546073463f6341cb411b21287f3e50b2b9733a6f39dfc53e1fb9ca5848\n- CD8 core_result.json: 50950f343600c9c0dd17ed5191a3511cc2fb397d1b026120f4fe47ca6ab1f102\n"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:56","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-abdf3758ddce3df7","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:20:12.353Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Gene Set on 3CA Choudhury 2022 Brain Data}\n\\author{}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of 1,988 metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether all cells form reproducible metabolic groupings using only these genes, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types and subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells. Results are described as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states but show descriptive associations with cell type and patient.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (Nature Genetics, 2022; DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8), available through the Curated Cancer Cell Atlas (3CA:20773). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes across 10 samples from 6 patients.\n\n\\subsection{Gene Set}\nA user-supplied CSV file containing 1,988 metabolic gene symbols was used. After exact-symbol matching against the gene list, 1,646 genes passed prevalence filtering (minimum 500 counts in at least 50\\% of cells).\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Preprocessing}\nCells were filtered for QC passing criteria. Expression was normalized using per-cell library size scaling to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    x_{ij}^{\\text{norm}} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{x_{ij}}{N_i} \\times 10^4\\right),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $x_{ij}$ is the UMI count for gene $j$ in cell $i$, and $N_i$ is the total UMI count for cell $i$. Features were scaled using \\texttt{scanpy.scale} with \\texttt{zero\\_center=False} and \\texttt{max\\_value=10}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution 0.8, selected as the resolution maximizing median silhouette score across 5 random seeds (0, 17, 42, 73, 101) with pairwise ARI $\\geq$ 0.75 as stability filter. PCA was performed with 30 components (arpack solver), followed by k-nearest neighbors graph construction with 15 neighbors.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Global Metabolic Grouping Reproducibility}\n\nUsing the user-supplied metabolic gene set on all 58,843 cells, we identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8. The clustering shows descriptive associations with cell type (NMI = 0.496) and cell subtype (NMI = 0.648), indicating that metabolic gene expression patterns are strongly influenced by cell identity.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP visualization of all cells colored by metabolic gene-derived cl\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 9958 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\n759).}\n\\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Random control comparison for CD8 T cell metabolic gene clustering.}\n\\label{tab:cd8_random_controls}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nMetabolic matched-kmeans silhouette & 0.095 \\\\\nRandom controls exceeding or equal & 17 \\\\\nRandom controls total & 19 \\\\\nAdd-one rank fraction & 0.9 \\\\\nHVG silhouette & 0.168 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Within-replicate sensitivity for CD8 T cell metabolic gene clustering.}\n\\label{tab:cd8_within_replicate}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nReplicates analyzed & 3 \\\\\nGlobal vs within ARI min & 0.511 \\\\\nGlobal vs within ARI max & 0.845 \\\\\nWeakest replicate & 6 \\\\\nWeakest replicate cells & 2,776 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\textbf{Conclusion for Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells, the metabolic gene set produces 6 clusters with low silhouette scores (0.099). The clustering shows associations with patient (NMI = 0.393) and sample (NMI = 0.443), indicating that sample-level factors influence metabolic gene expression. The analysis concludes as \\textit{inconclusive} for discrete metabolic states within CD8 T cells, as clustering scores are comparable to random controls (add-one rank fraction = 0.9).\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\nThe analysis of metabolic genes on the Choudhury 2022 meningioma dataset shows that metabolic gene expression patterns are strongly associated with cell type and cell subtype. The clustering does not demonstrate discrete metabolic states independent of cell type identity. Within CD8 T cells, metabolic patterns show associations with patient and sample, suggesting that sample-level factors influence metabolic gene expression.\n\nThese findings are consistent with the expectation that metabolic gene expression is cell-type-specific rather than defining independent metabolic states. The inconclusive nature of the clustering (comparable to random controls) suggests that the user-supplied metabolic gene set may not capture biologically meaningful metabolic states in this dataset.\n\n\\section{Limitations}\n\n1. The analysis concludes as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states, as clustering scores are comparable to random controls.\n2. Cell-level counts are not independent biological replicates; cells are nested within patients and samples.\n3. The analysis is descriptive and does not test for discrete biological states.\n4. Confounding by cell type, patient, and sample is evident in the clustering results.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nChoudhury, A., Magill, S. T., Eaton, C. D., et al.\nMeningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\n\\bibitem{co_scientist}\nCo-Scientist.\nGenerative, reflective, and meta-reviewing for scientific research.\n\\textit{Nature Methods}, 2023.\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"write_file:f4faab36e962c1dd5b274c6f4ef71f0c487a6c583a21d526853c3bb8565e7b88","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7be91b09e0b71f4","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:23:41.498Z","result":"Wrote 11488 bytes to report\\main.tex"},{"key":"build_research_report:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a:57","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88310eb2bd1ec916","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:23:50.521Z","result":"ERROR: {\"error\": \"pdflatex pass 1 failed with exit code 1:\\nThis is pdfTeX, Version 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.29 (MiKTeX 26.5) (preloaded format=pdflatex.fmt)\\n restricted \\\\wri