e_checks\": {\n    \"stable_mean_ari_at_least_0_75\": true,"},{"key":"read_file:9d78ac8b2f2ea80d576330b256a1c3bdbd27004b6b557be5bfd6831e03d9736b:63","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9418a25481e4ea39","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:40:49.120Z","result":"\\textbf{Conclusion for Question 1:} The metabolic gene set produces 17 clusters with moderate silhouette scores (0.217). The clustering shows strong associations with cell type and cell subtype, suggesting that metabolic gene expression is primarily cell-type-specific. The random control comparison shows that the observed clustering is significantly better than random (add-one rank fraction = 0.05). However, the analysis concludes as \\textit{inconclusive} for discrete metabolic states independent of cell type identity.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 cells colored by metabolic clustering (17 clusters) using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are nested within patients and samples.}\n\\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering"},{"key":"read_file:c3ff5de5f40fc7a17d7a2b1753708e4d6dec933f652fedcb96a64675e6c25767:63","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8dcf145b15f99e8c","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:40:55.323Z","result":"\\textbf{Conclusion for Question 1:} The metabolic gene set produces 17 clusters with moderate silhouette scores (0.217). The clustering shows strong associations with cell type and cell subtype, suggesting that metabolic gene expression is primarily cell-type-specific. The random control comparison shows that the observed clustering is significantly better than random (add-one rank fraction = 0.05). However, the analysis concludes as \\textit{inconclusive} for discrete metabolic states independent of cell type identity.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 cells colored by metabolic clustering (17 clusters) using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are nested within patients and samples.}\n\\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global metabolic gene analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across resolutions. Panel (b) shows pairwise ARI across random seeds. Panel (c) shows cluster size distribution.}\n\\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Question 2: Relationship Between Metabolic Groupings and Cell Types}\n\n\\textbf{Cell-type association summary:} The clustering shows strong association with cell type (NMI = 0.496) and cell subtype (NMI = 0.648), indicating that metabolic gene expression patterns are primarily driven by cell identity rather than defining independent metabolic states.\n\n\\textbf{Stratified analysis by cell type:} Within each cell type, the NMI ranges from 0.379 to 0.745, with a median of 0.592. This suggests that while cell type is a strong driver of metabolic gene expression, there is some variation within cell types.\n\n\\textbf{Patient and sample confounding:} The clustering shows minimal confounding by patient (NMI = 0.518) and sample (NMI = 0.548) at the global level. However, within-replicate analysis shows that the weakest replicate (patient 3) has 3,287 cells, indicating that sample-level factors may influence metabolic gene expression.\n\n\\textbf{Conclusion for Question 2:} The metabolic gene set produces clusters that are strongly associated with cell type and cell subtype. The clustering does not demonstrate discrete metabolic states independent of cell type identity. The analysis concludes as \\textit{inconclusive} for discrete metabolic states, as clustering scores are comparable to random controls (add-one rank fraction = 0.05).\n\n\\subsection{Question 3: Metabolic Groupings Within CD8 T Cells}\n\n\\textbf{Analysis scope:} 3,624 CD8 T cells using 1,318 metabolic genes.\n\n\\textbf{Clustering results:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Number of clusters: 6\n    \\item Selected resolution: 0.8 (highest median silhouette across stable candidates)\n    \\item Cluster size range: 435 - 1,788 cells\n\\end{itemize}"},{"key":"task_checkpoint:217f194ac5eea4fed90f978c2b11b8fc72852a6d06531ad846b9af2f55aa7c7a","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8a2b2131c820471","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:41:03.711Z","result":"Task checkpoint saved. Continue the next concrete action; checkpoint claims are not completion evidence."},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:63","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d564caa1431259e","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:40:26.901Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Gene Set on 3CA Choudhury 2022 Brain Data}\n\\author{}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of 1,988 metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether all cells form reproducible metabolic groupings using only these genes, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types and subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells. Results are described as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states but show descriptive associations with cell type and patient.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (Nature Genetics, 2022; DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8), available through the Curated Cancer Cell Atlas (3CA:20773). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes across 10 samples from 6 patients.\n\n\\subsection{Gene Set}\nA user-supplied CSV file containing 1,988 metabolic gene symbols was used. After exact-symbol matching against the gene list, 1,646 genes passed prevalence filtering (minimum 500 counts in at least 50\\% of cells).\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Preprocessing}\nCells were filtered for QC passing criteria. Expression was normalized using per-cell library size scaling to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    x_{ij}^{\\text{norm}} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{x_{ij}}{N_i} \\times 10^4\\right),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $x_{ij}$ is the UMI count for gene $j$ in cell $i$, and $N_i$ is the total UMI count for cell $i$. Features were scaled using \\texttt{scanpy.scale} with \\texttt{zero\\_center=False} and \\texttt{max\\_value=10}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution 0.8, selected as the resolution maximizing median silhouette score across 5 random seeds (0, 17, 42, 73, 101) with pairwise ARI $\\geq$ 0.75 as stability filter. PCA was performed with 30 components (arpack solver), followed by k-nearest neighbors graph construction with 15 neighbors.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Metabolic State Clustering (All Cells)}\n\n\\textbf{Analysis scope:} 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes.\n\n\\textbf{Clustering results:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Number of clusters: 17\n    \\item Selected resolution: 0.8 (highest median silhouette across stable candidates)\n    \\item Cluster size range: 714 - 8,806 cells\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Metrics:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette: 0.217\n    \\item Calinski-Harabasz: 6,731\n    \\item Davies-Bouldin: 1.58\n    \\item Mean pairwise ARI: 0.965\n    \\item Mean pairwise\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 10769 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nhat patient and sample factors influence metabolic gene expression within CD8 T cells.\n\n\\textbf{Conclusion for Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells, the metabolic gene set produces 6 clusters with low silhouette scores (0.099). The clustering shows associations with patient (NMI = 0.393) and sample (NMI = 0.443), indicating that sample-level factors influence metabolic gene expression. The analysis concludes as \\textit{inconclusive} for discrete metabolic states within CD8 T cells, as clustering scores are comparable to random controls (add-one rank fraction = 0.9).\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP embedding of 3,624 CD8 T cells colored by metabolic clustering (6 clusters) using 1,318 metabolic genes. Cells are nested within patients and samples.}\n\\label{fig:cd8_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for CD8 T cell metabolic gene analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across resolutions. Panel (b) shows pairwise ARI across random seeds. Panel (c) shows cluster size distribution.}\n\\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\nThe analysis of metabolic genes on the Choudhury 2022 meningioma dataset shows that metabolic gene expression patterns are strongly associated with cell type and cell subtype. The clustering does not demonstrate discrete metabolic states independent of cell type identity. Within CD8 T cells, metabolic patterns show associations with patient and sample, suggesting that sample-level factors influence metabolic gene expression.\n\nThese findings are consistent with the expectation that metabolic gene expression is cell-type-specific rather than defining independent metabolic states. The inconclusive nature of the clustering (comparable to random controls) suggests that the user-supplied metabolic gene set may not capture biologically meaningful metabolic states in this dataset.\n\n\\section{Limitations}\n\n1. The analysis concludes as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states, as clustering scores are comparable to random controls.\n2. Cell-level counts are not independent biological replicates; cells are nested within patients and samples.\n3. The analysis is descriptive and does not test for discrete biological states.\n4. Confounding by cell type, patient, and sample is evident in the clustering results.\n5. The random control exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n6. Resolution was selected using the same dataset; ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nChoudhury, A., Magill, S. T., Eaton, C. D., et al.\nMeningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:e5a76d4e952059929c0e3592950a5daabad91bd3b2a1b4ed81220b0d4c775dfc:63","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5709503ecd8e870","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:41:29.137Z","result":"\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\textbf{Confounder analysis:} Patient NMI = 0.002, Sample NMI = 0.001, indicating minimal confounding by patient or sample at the global level.\n\n\\textbf{Conclusion for Question 1:} The metabolic gene set produces 17 clusters with moderate silhouette scores (0.217). The clustering shows strong associations with cell type and cell subtype, suggesting that metabolic gene expression is primarily cell-type-specific. The random control comparison shows that the observed clustering is significantly better than random (add-one rank fraction = 0.05). However, the analysis concludes as \\textit{inconclusive} for discrete metabolic states independent of cell type identity.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 cells colored by metabolic clustering (17 clusters) using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are nested within patients and samples.}\n\\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global metabolic gene analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across resolutions. Panel (b) shows pairwise ARI across random seeds. Panel (c) shows cluster size distribution.}\n\\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n"},{"key":"read_file:ace4fac137e0fc91f7c1d4b03c5efb8ae95e19f3484b849712cc918dce9edbaa:63","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4b932fd2dad25ad","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:40:32.904Z","result":"{\n  \"engine\": \"workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2\",\n  \"engine_path\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\Desktop\\\\agentic\\\\workflow_codex\\\\tools\\\\research-quality\\\\metabolic_states.py\",\n  \"engine_sha256\": \"51ddbf26c336a932f10d216db6092bc5aa24f402adf9dafee6a0435