L�4о<~Q���N���$\tKN��zӐҺL�\u0018�ղ(ǠQ-��u8���pct��\u0014����x���6ޓ?��Y!F?\u001c\u000e��{\u0016C&\\)1\u0006��\u000b/���\u0011�^��\u001f��~cZC�\nendstream\nendobj\n16 0 obj\n<< /Type /XObject /Subtype /Form /BBox [ -8 -8 8 8 ] /Length 131\n/Filter /FlateDecode >>\nstream\nx�m�A\u000e� \fE�=E/�IKEe��k��L���\u0003q@L�4о<~Q���N���$\tKN��zӐҺL�\u0018�ղ(ǠQ-��u8���pct��\u0014����x���6ޓ?��Y!F?\u001c\u000e��{\u0016C&\\)1\u0006��\u000b/���\u0011�^��\u001f��~cZC�\nendstream\nendobj\n17 0 obj\n<< /Type /XObject /Subtype /Form /BBox [ -8 -8 8 8 ] /Length 131\n/Filter /FlateDecode >>\nstream\nx�m�A\u000e� \fE�=E/�IKEe��k��L���\u0003q@L�4о<~Q���N���$\tKN��zӐҺL�\u0018�ղ(ǠQ-��u8���pct��\u0014����x���6ޓ?��Y!F?\u001c\u000e��{\u0016C&\\)1\u0006��\u000b/���\u0011�^��\u001f��~cZC�\nendstream\nendobj\n2 0 obj\n<< /Type /Pages /Kids [ 11 0 R ] /Count 1 >>\nendobj\n25 0 obj\n<< /Creator (Matplotlib v3.11.2, https://matplotlib.org)\n/Producer (Matplotlib pdf backend v3.11.2)\n/CreationDate (D:20260913231541+08'00') >>\nendobj\nxref\n0 26\n0000000000 65535 f \n0000000016 00000 n \n0000016312 00000 n \n0000014750 00000 n \n0000014782 00000 n \n0000014924 00000 n \n0000014945 00000 n \n0000014966 00000 n \n0000000065 00000 n \n0000000347 00000 n \n0000004713 00000 n \n0000000208 00000 n \n0000004692 00000 n \n0000015042 00000 n \n0000015296 00000 n \n0000015550 00000 n \n0000015804 00000 n \n0000016058 00000 n \n0000014258 00000 n \n0000013898 00000 n \n0000014111 00000 n \n0000013298 00000 n \n0000004733 00000 n \n0000014482 00000 n \n0000013463 00000 n \n0000016372 00000 n \ntrailer\n<< /Size 26 /Root 1 0 R /Info 25 0 R >>\nstartxref\n16531\n%%EOF\n"},{"key":"read_file:e1d112006a4a726f80691b10ea60033fc27313219f3522680c67a4cb69df93c5:71","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83ae0c36a5227003","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:58:52.947Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Gene Set on 3CA Choudhury 2022 Brain Data}\n\\author{}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of 1,988 metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether all cells form reproducible metabolic groupings using only these genes, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types and subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells. Results are described as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states but show descriptive associations with cell type and patient.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (Nature Genetics, 2022; DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8), available through the Curated Cancer Cell Atlas (3CA:20773). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes across 10 samples from 6 patients.\n\n\\subsection{Gene Set}\nA user-supplied CSV file containing 1,988 metabolic gene symbols was used. After exact-symbol matching against the gene list, 1,646 genes passed prevalence filtering (minimum 500 counts in at least 50\\% of cells).\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Preprocessing}\nCells were filtered for QC passing criteria. Expression was normalized using per-cell library size scaling to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    x_{ij}^{\\text{norm}} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{x_{ij}}{N_i} \\times 10^4\\right),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $x_{ij}$ is the UMI count for gene $j$ in cell $i$, and $N_i$ is the total UMI count for cell $i$. Features were scaled using \\texttt{scanpy.scale} with \\texttt{zero\\_center=False} and \\texttt{max\\_value=10}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution 0.8, selected as the resolution maximizing median silhouette score across 5 random seeds (0, 17, 42, 73, 101) with pairwise ARI $\\geq$ 0.75 as stability filter. PCA was performed with 30 components (arpack solver), followed by k-nearest neighbors graph construction with 15 neighbors.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Metabolic State Clustering (All Cells)}\n\n\\textbf{Analysis scope:} 58,843 cells analyzed using 1,646 metabolic genes.\n\n\\textbf{Clustering results:} 17 clusters were identified at resolution 0.8. Cluster sizes range from 714 to 8,806 cells.\n\n\\textbf{Stability:} The clustering is stable across seeds with mean pairwise ARI = 0.965 and median silhouette = 0.222.\n\n\\textbf{Random control comparison:} Of 19 random gene controls, 2 exceeded or equal the metabolic clustering silhouette (add-one rank fraction = 0.15). This is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n\n\\textbf{Confounder analysis:} The clustering shows strong association with cell type (NMI = 0.496) and cell subtype (NMI = 0.648). Patient NMI = 0.518 and sample NMI = 0.548 indicate confounding by sample.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Global clustering metrics for all cells using metabolic genes}\n\\label{tab:global_metrics}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nCells analyzed & 58,843 \\\\\nMetabolic genes analyzed & 1,646 \\\\\nNumber of clusters & 17 \\\\\nSelected resolution & 0.8 \\\\\nSilhouette score & 0.217 \\\\\nMean pairwise ARI & 0.965 \\\\\nCell type NMI & 0.496 \\\\\nCell subtype NMI & 0.648 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 cells colored by metabolic clustering (17 clusters) using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are nested within patients and samples.}\n\\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global metabolic gene analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across resolutions. Panel (b) shows pairwise ARI across random seeds. Panel (c) shows cluster size distribution.}\n\\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Question 2: Relationship Between Metabolic Clusters and Cell Types}\n\n\\textbf{Cell type association:} The global clustering shows moderate association with cell type (NMI = 0.496). Within each of 6 patients, the within-replicate NMI ranges from 0.379 to 0.745, with median = 0.592.\n\n\\textbf{Cell subtype association:} Stronger association with cell subtype (NMI = 0.648). Within each of 6 patients, the within-replicate NMI ranges from 0.224 to 0.680, with median = 0.636.\n\n\\textbf{Patient/sample confounding:} Patient NMI = 0.518 and sample NMI = 0.548 indicate that clustering is confounded by patient and sample. The within-replicate ARI ranges from 0.071 to 0.676 across 6 patients, with patient 3 being the weakest replicate (3,287 cells).\n\n\\textbf{Interpretation:} Metabolic gene expression patterns are strongly associated with cell type and cell subtype identity. The clustering does not demonstrate discrete metabolic states independent of cell type. The strong confounding by patient and sample suggests that sample-level factors influence metabolic gene expression patterns.\n\n\\subsection{Question 3: Metabolic States Within CD8 T Cells}\n"},{"key":"read_file:ace4fac137e0fc91f7c1d4b03c5efb8ae95e19f3484b849712cc918dce9edbaa:71","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b99f54b3ac56cec","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":