scriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\",\n    \"Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\",\n    \"A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\",\n    \"Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\",\n    \"Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\"\n  ]\n}\n"},{"key":"read_file:b465f7e184073134d4e0e47755d604125d5b6f44def47057073a4561588b7587:71","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ba202bbd8ae496bd","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:59:48.857Z","result":"Cell subtype NMI & 0.648 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 cells colored by metabolic clustering (17 clusters) using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are nested within patients and samples.}\n\\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global metabolic gene analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across resolutions. Panel (b) shows pairwise ARI across random seeds. Panel (c) shows cluster size distribution.}"},{"key":"read_file:74c64ff2392719cebb6d001bf4c131676cee2abe1992cdfbcc67310c9e044573:71","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b400c0b89a539b4c","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:59:52.307Z","result":"{\n  \"engine\": \"workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2\",\n  \"engine_path\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\Desktop\\\\agentic\\\\workflow_codex\\\\tools\\\\research-quality\\\\metabolic_states.py\",\n  \"engine_sha256\": \"51ddbf26c336a932f10d216db6092bc5aa24f402adf9dafee6a0435382592401\",\n  \"methods\": {\n    \"unit\": \"Exact-symbol aggregated raw counts; all source features form each cell library-size denominator\",\n    \"normalization\": \"Per-cell library size to 10000, then log1p\",\n    \"feature_scaling\": \"Scanpy scale(zero_center=False, max_value=10)\",\n    \"pca\": {\n      \"solver\": \"arpack\",\n      \"zero_center\": true,\n      \"components\": 30,\n      \"seed\": 0\n    },\n    \"neighbors\": {\n      \"n_neighbors\": 15,\n      \"seed\": 0\n    },\n    \"leiden\": {\n      \"flavor\": \"igraph\",\n      \"n_iterations\": 2,\n      \"directed\": false\n    },\n    \"HVG_control\": \"Highest variance of library-normalized log1p expression among prevalence-passing non-mitochondrial symbols; no dispersion/mean bin normalization\",\n    \"random_controls\": \"Size-matched sampling without replacement from prevalence-passing non-metabolic, non-mitochondrial symbols\",\n    \"control_partition\": {\n      \"method\": \"KMeans\",\n      \"n_init\": 20,\n      \"clusters\": 17,\n      \"seed\": 0\n    },\n    \"metrics\": {\n      \"space\": \"PCA\",\n      \"silhouette_subsample_seed\": 7321,\n      \"silhouette_cells\": 3000,\n      \"CH_DBI_population\": \"All QC-passing cells\"\n    },\n    \"stability\": \"Leiden randomization on a fixed PCA/neighbor graph; resolution chosen on the same dataset\",\n    \"resolution_selection\": {\n      \"stability_filter\": \"mean_pairwise_ari >= 0.75\",\n      \"stable_candidates\": [\n        0.4,\n        0.8,\n        1.2\n      ],\n      \"fallback_to_all_resolutions\": false,\n      \"primary_criterion\": \"highest median silhouette across seeds\",\n      \"tie_breaker\": \"highest mean_pairwise_ari\",\n      \"selected_resolution\": 0.8\n    }\n  },\n  \"inputs\": {\n    \"expression_path\": \"inputs/Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx\",\n    \"expression_sha256\": \"51bf0452d166511fecca3f32d89f9fd0f631d173143173d82115f49cdaaa6c1d\",\n    \"cells_path\": \"inputs/Cells.csv\",\n    \"cells_sha256\": \"459fec35fbd3c89536bab26add4f3b733b4d57ce1deb08ad591455df68b94d37\",\n    \"genes_path\": \"inputs/Genes.txt\",\n    \"genes_sha256\": \"a285759694b2e6fdb495fa4e996643b50e77588013dbba7b1eb3d1926bd95b60\",\n    \"matrix_orientation\": \"genes_by_cells\",\n    \"matrix_shape\": [\n      33538,\n      58843\n    ],\n    \"metabolic_genes_path\": \"inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv\",\n    \"metabolic_genes_sha256\": \"7503180d29693bd1e654ea93281db0e3ec4177ff44842c8b02f6a098402e41a9\",\n    \"gene_set_audit\": {\n      \"format\": \"csv\",\n      \"nonempty_rows\": 1988,\n      \"unique_exact_symbols\": 1988,\n      \"duplicate_exact_rows\": 0,\n      \"casefold_collision_groups\": 0,\n      \"exact_matches\": 341,\n      \"case_insensitive_unique_matches\": 1457,\n      \"unmatched_symbols\": 190,\n      \"ambiguous_casefold_symbols\": 0,\n      \"duplicate_expression_mappings\": 0,\n      \"matched_expression_symbols_before_prevalence\": 1798,\n      \"included_expression_symbols_after_prevalence\": 1646\n    },\n    \"subset\": {\n      \"field\": null,\n      \"values\": [],\n      \"source_cells_selected\": 58843\n    }\n  },\n  \"parameters\": {\n    \"seeds\": [\n      0,\n      17,\n      42,\n      73,\n      101\n    ],\n    \"resolutions\": [\n      0.4,\n      0.8,\n      1.2\n    ],\n    \"random_baselines\": 19,\n    \"min_counts\": 500,"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:71","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-90027f9fb1a12009","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T17:58:34.386Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Gene Set on 3CA Choudhury 2022 Brain Data}\n\\author{}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of 1,988 metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether all cells form reproducible metabolic groupings using only these genes, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types and subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells. Results are described as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states but show descriptive associations with cell type and patient.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (Nature Genetics, 2022; DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8), available through the Curated Cancer Cell Atlas (3CA:20773). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes across 10 samples from 6 patients.\n\n\\subsection{Gene Set}\nA user-supplied CSV file containing 1,988 metabolic gene symbols was used. After exact-symbol matching against the gene list, 1,646 genes passed prevalence filtering (minimum 500 counts in at least 50\\% of cells).\n\n\\subsection{Normalization and Preprocessing}\nCells were filtered for QC passing criteria. Expression was normalized using per-cell library size scaling to 10,000 UMI counts followed by log1p transformation:\n\\begin{equation}\n    x_{ij}^{\\text{norm}} = \\log_1\\left(\\frac{x_{ij}}{N_i} \\times 10^4\\right),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $x_{ij}$ is the UMI count for gene $j$ in cell $i$, and $N_i$ is the total UMI count for cell $i$. Features were scaled using \\texttt{scanpy.scale} with \\texttt{zero\\_center=False} and \\texttt{max\\_value=10}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution 0.8, selected as the resolution maximizing median silhouette score across 5 random seeds (0, 17, 42, 73, 101) with pairwise ARI $\\geq$ 0.75 as stability filter. PCA was performed with 30 components (arpack solver), followed by k-nearest neighbors graph construction with 15 neighbors.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Metabolic State Clustering (All Cells)}\n\n\\textbf{Analysis scope:} 58,843 cells analyzed using 1,646 metabolic genes.\n\n\\textbf{Clustering results:} 17 clusters were identified at resolution 0.8. Cluster sizes range from 714 to 8,806 cells.\n\n\\textbf{Stability:} The clustering is stable across seeds with mean pairwise ARI = 0.965 and median silhouette = 0.222.\n\n\\textbf{Random control comparison:} Of 19 random gene controls, 2 exceeded or equal the metabolic clustering silhouette (add-one rank fraction = 0.15). This is a d\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 9038 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nbiological P value.\n\n\\textbf{Confounder analysis:} Patient NMI = 0.393 and sample NMI = 0.443 indicate confounding by patient and sample.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{CD8 T cell clustering metrics using metabolic genes}\n\\label{tab:cd8_metrics}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nCells analyzed & 3,624 \\\\\nMetabolic genes analyzed & 1,318 \\\\\nNumber of clusters & 6 \\\\\nSelected resolution & 0.4 \\\\\nSilhouette score & 0.099 \\\\\nMean pairwise ARI & 0.759 \\\\\nPatient NMI & 0.393 \\\\\nSample NMI & 0.443 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP embedding of 3,624 CD8 T cells colored by metabolic clustering (6 clusters) using 1,318 metabolic genes. Cells are nested within patients and samples.}\n\\label{fig:cd8_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for CD8 T cell metabolic gene analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across resolutions. Panel (b) shows pairwise ARI across random seeds. Panel (c) shows cluster size distribution.}\n\\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\nThe analysis of metabolic genes on the Choudhury 2022 meningioma dataset shows that metabolic gene expression patterns are strongly associated with cell type and cell subtype. The clustering does not demonstrate discrete metabolic states independent of cell type identity. Within CD8 T cells, metabolic patterns show associations with patient and sample, suggesting that sample-level factors influence metabolic gene expression.\n\nThese findings are consistent with the expectation that metabolic gene expression is cell-type-specific rather than defining independent metabolic states. The inconclusive nature of the clustering (comparable to random controls) suggests that the user-supplied metabolic gene set may not capture biologically meaningful metabolic states in this dataset.\n\n\\section{Limitations}\n\n1. The analysis concludes as inconclusive for discrete metabolic states, as clustering scores are comparable to random controls.\n2. Cell-level counts are not independent biological replicates; cells are nested within patients and samples.\n3. The analysis is descriptive and does not test for discrete biological states.\n4. Confounding by cell type, patient, and sample is evident in the clustering results.\n5. The random control exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n6. Resolution was selected using the same dataset; ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nChoudhury, A., Magill, S. T., Eaton, C. D., et al.\nMeningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"write_file:40be9a984ee1a54786a60a189b0c3cacfdb2b3513138b5be092df5a626a03fd6","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc026096b42b6086","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:00:00.973Z","result":"Wrote 118 bytes to check_figures.ps1"},{"key":"build_research_report:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a:72","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84cb767ae6b39d7c","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:00:51.070Z","result":"ERROR: {\"error\": \"pdflatex pass 1 failed with exit code 1:\\nThis is pdfTeX, Version 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.29 (MiKTeX 26.5) (preloaded format=pdflatex.fmt)\\n restricted \\\\write18 enabled.\\nentering extended mode\\n(main.tex\\nLaTeX2e <2026-06-01>\\nL3 programming layer <2026-06-18>\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/base\\\\article.cls\\nDocument Class: article 2025/01/22 v1.4n Standard LaTeX document class\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/base\\\\size11.clo))\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/base\\\\inputenc.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/booktabs\\\\booktabs.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/graphics\\\\graphicx.sty\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\t