00022538 00000 n \n0000035125 00000 n \n0000046336 00000 n \n0000012639 00000 n \n0000012279 00000 n \n0000012492 00000 n \n0000011679 00000 n \n0000004098 00000 n \n0000012863 00000 n \n0000011836 00000 n \n0000013364 00000 n \n0000022517 00000 n \n0000025862 00000 n \n0000025883 00000 n \n0000035104 00000 n \n0000037055 00000 n \n0000037076 00000 n \n0000046315 00000 n \n0000053888 00000 n \n0000053969 00000 n \ntrailer\n<< /Size 33 /Root 1 0 R /Info 32 0 R >>\nstartxref\n54128\n%%EOF\n"},{"key":"read_file:6f5f044c9e33caed6a24fee10e18c136a2efb976d1bae27a5f4d971502942bef:85","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9176c4317d9162c8","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:47:40.112Z","result":"\n\\subsubsection{Within-Replicate Summary}\nThe resolution selection used a mean pairwise ARI $\\ge$ 0.75 filter. The median silhouette criterion was used for resolution selection, with ARI as the tie-breaker.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP embedding of 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are colored by the 17 metabolic clusters identified at resolution 0.8.}\n\\label{fig:metabolic_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n"},{"key":"read_file:c63d0be4b238f04cf533c9557adcf6ebf1d2a7ec74228ba937e760e2f7d5e56b:85","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b89580a8c0065fd","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:47:48.637Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Metabolic State Analysis Using Single-Cell RNA-Seq}\n\\author{Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nThis analysis examines transcriptional metabolic states in single-cell RNA-seq data using the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset (3CA:20773) with user-supplied metabolic genes.\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\\subsection{Data Processing}\nThe analysis uses the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset from the Curated Cancer Cell Atlas (3CA:20773). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes from 10 meningioma samples.\n\nUser-supplied metabolic genes from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv} were used for clustering. The input file contains 1,988 source gene symbols, with 1,646 matched to expression features after exact and case-insensitive matching.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were processed using the following normalization pipeline:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{normalized}_{ij} = \\log_1p\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI}_{ij}}{\\text{library}_i} \\times 10000\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\text{UMI}_{ij}$ is the UMI count for gene $j$ in cell $i$, $\\text{library}_i$ is the total UMI count for cell $i$, and $\\log_1p$ is the natural logarithm of $x+1$.\n\nFeatures were scaled using Scanpy's \\texttt{scale} method with \\texttt{zero\\_center=False} and \\texttt{max\\_value=10}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with the following parameters:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item PCA: 30 components, arpack solver, seed=0\n    \\item Neighbors: 15 neighbors, seed=0\n    \\item Leiden: igraph flavor, 2 iterations, undirected graph\n\\end{itemize}\n\nResolution was selected using a stability filter of \\texttt{mean\\_pairwise\\_ari $\\ge$ 0.75} across multiple seeds, with the primary criterion being highest median silhouette and tie-breaking by highest mean pairwise ARI.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Question 1: Global Metabolic State Clustering}\n\\subsubsection{Analysis Overview}\nUsing all 58,843 cells and 1,646 metabolic genes, the analysis identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cluster size summary for global metabolic state analysis}\n\\label{tab:global_clusters}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nTotal cells analyzed & 58,843 \\\\\nMetabolic genes analyzed & 1,646 \\\\\nNumber of clusters & 17 \\\\\nSelected resolution & 0.8 \\\\\nMinimum cluster size & 714 cells \\\\\nMaximum cluster size & 8,806 cells \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Clustering Metrics}\nThe clustering achieved a silhouette score of 0.217 and a mean pairwise ARI of 0.965 across seeds. The HVG-based control achieved a silhouette of 0.283, while random gene controls (19 controls) had mean silhouette of 0.210.\n\n\\subsubsection{Cell-Type Association}\nThe categorical confounder NMI for cell\\_type is 0.496, a\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 10235 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\n_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\subsection{Conclusions}\n\\textbf{Question 1:} Using all cells and metabolic genes, the analysis identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8. However, the clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.217) are comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.210), and the confounder NMI for patient (0.518) and sample (0.548) indicates that clustering is partially driven by batch effects. The results are descriptive and do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\textbf{Question 2:} Metabolic clusters show moderate association with cell types (NMI: 0.496) and cell subtypes (NMI: 0.648), but the clustering is not dominated by these factors alone. The patient and sample confounders also contribute significantly.\n\n\\textbf{Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells, 6 clusters were identified at resolution 0.4. However, the clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.099) are lower than the HVG control (0.168) and comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.107). The add-one rank fraction of 0.9 indicates that most random controls performed similarly or better.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette is estimated on a fixed random subset; all QC-passing cells are retained for PCA, graph construction, and clustering.\n    \\item The unsupervised question has no predefined condition contrast; no cell-level differential-expression P values are reported.\n    \\item No significance test is performed on data-derived cluster labels; within-replicate reclustering is descriptive and not held-out donor validation.\n    \\item Random gene controls are matched in size, not expression mean or dispersion; their exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\n    \\item A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\n    \\item Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\n    \\item Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, et al.\n``Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.''\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\n\\bibitem{co_scientist}\nNature.\n``Co-Scientist: A framework for scientific reasoning and meta-review.''\n\\textit{Nature}, 2023.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41586-023-06130-4}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:735e41b467f436ed1f3678340be14bde4a62c98275368bd6b8120eb244967b49:85","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b68c979b2661d370","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:47:56.565Z","result":"{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"dataset\": {\n    \"study_id\": \"3ca:20773\",\n    \"source_url\": \"https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/4y8x28n9v6y5bkss3mqgu/Data_Choudhury2022_Brain.tar.gz?rlkey=3jhc85s8p3tp5jatdrauh6ka7&dl=1\",\n    \"sha256\": \"2156a73fd5f95a198ef90821f2d734bfa7e0b5aa3686deefd1b5c5d5d1269aa1\",\n    \"expression_path\": \"inputs/Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx\",\n    \"cells_path\": \"inputs/Cells.csv\",\n    \"gene_names_path\": \"inputs/Genes.txt\",\n    \"n_cells\": 58843,\n    \"n_genes\": 33538,\n    \"matrix_orientation\": \"genes_by_cells\",\n    \"cell_id_column\": \"cell_name\"\n  },\n  \"feature_set\": {\n    \"name\": \"User-supplied metabolic genes\",\n    \"source_url\": \"local-user-supplied-input\",\n    \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-13T15:15:45.001Z\",\n    \"sha256\": \"7503180d29693bd1e654ea93281db0e3ec4177ff44842c8b02f6a098402e41a9\",\n    \"genes_path\": \"inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv\",\n    \"matched_genes_path\": \"results/core_analysis/matched_metabolic_genes.txt\",\n    \"source_gene_count\": 1988,\n    \"matched_gene_count\": 1646\n  },\n  \"analysis\": {\n    \"engine\": \"workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2\",\n    \"core_result_path\": \"results/core_analysis/core_result.json\",\n    \"core_result_sha256\": \"0da19b546073463f6341cb411b21287f3e50b2b9733a6f39dfc53e1fb9ca5848\",\n    \"input_unit\": \"cell\",\n    \"rows_analyzed\": 58843,\n    \"unique_cells_analyzed\": 58843,\n    \"random_seeds\": [0],\n    \"baselines\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"HVG and random size-matched controls\",\n        \"metrics\": {\n          \"random_controls_exceeding_or_equal\": 2,\n          \"random_controls_total\": 19,\n          \"add_one_rank_fraction\": 0.15\n        },\n        \"result_path\": \"results/core_analysis/baseline_metrics.csv\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"confounders_checked\": [\"patient\", \"cell_type\", \"sample\", \"source\", \"cell_subtype\", \"cell_cycle_phase\", \"complexity\", \"computed_total_counts\", \"computed_detected_genes\", \"computed_mito_percent\"],\n    \"methods\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Scanpy Leiden on supplied metabolic genes\",\n        \"labels_path\": \"results/core_analysis/cluster_labels.csv\",\n        \"cell_id_column\": \"cell_name\",\n        \"label_column\": \"cluster\",\n        \"n_clusters\": 17,\n        \"metrics\": {\n          \"silhouette\": 0.21661581099033356,\n          \"mean_pairwise_ari\": 0.9650730581380069,\n          \"median_silhouette\": 0.22155894339084625\n        }\n      }\n    ],\n    \"subgroup_analyses\": [\n      {\n        \"scope\": {\n          \"field\": \"cell_subtype\",\n          \"values\": [\"CD8 T cells\"]\n        },\n        \"core_result_path\": \"results/cd8_analysis/core_result.json\",\n        \"core_result_sha256\": \"50950f343600c9c0dd17ed5191a3511cc2fb397d1b026120f4fe47ca6ab1f102\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"key\": \"choudhury_2022\",\n      \"doi\": \"10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8\",\n      \"metadata_path\": \"sources/literature/crossref_db798362115f799a.json\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"artifacts\": {\n    \"tex\": \"report/main.tex\",\n    \"pdf\": \"report/main.pdf\",\n    \"summary\": \"results/summary.json\",\n    \"figures\": [\n      \"figures/metabolic_umap.pdf\",\n      \"figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf\",\n      \"figures/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf\",\n      \"figures/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n"},{"key":"read_file:73b32307891b34e87e0219a23860b0496d0e637e87884306d3ad6467f9e2693d:85","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b364719384d2471","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:48:33.926Z","result":"# QA_R4: Metabolic State Analysis of Meningioma Single-Cell Data\n\n## Overview\n\nThis repository contains the analysis of transcriptional metabolic states using user-supplied metabolic genes on the Choudhury et al. 2022 brain single-cell RNA-seq dataset (3CA:20773).\n\n## Dataset\n\n- **Study**: Choudhury et al. 2022 - \"Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities\"\n- **3CA ID**: 3ca:20773\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8\n- **Technology**: 10x Genomics\n- **Samples**: 10\n- **Total cells**: 58,843\n\n## Data Sources\n\n### 3CA Raw Count Data\n- **Archive**: Data_Choudhury2022_Brain.tar.gz\n- **SHA-256**: 2156a73fd5f95a198ef90821f2d734bfa7e0b5aa3686deefd1b5c5d5d1269aa1\n- **Expression matrix**: inputs/Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx (33,538 genes × 58,843 cells)\n- **Cells metadata**: inputs/Cells.csv\n- **Genes list**: inputs/Genes.txt\n\n### User-Supplied Metabolic Genes\n- **File**: inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv\n- **SHA-256**: 7503180d29693bd1e654ea93281db0e3ec4177ff44842c8b02f6a098402e41a9\n- **Source gene count**: 1,988 symbols\n- **Matched genes after prevalence filtering**: 1,646 (global analysis), 1,318 (CD8 T cells subset)\n\n## Analysis Results\n\n### Question 1: Metabolic State Clustering (All Cells)\n\n**Analysis scope**: 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes\n\n**Clustering results**:\n- **Number of clusters**: 17\n- **Selected resolution**: 0.8 (highest median silhouette across stable candidates)\n- **Cluster size range**: 714 - 8,806 cells\n\n**Metrics**:\n- Silhouette: 0.217\n- Calinski-Harabasz: 6,731\n- Davies-Bouldin: 1.58\n- Mean pairwise ARI: 0.965\n- Mean pairwise NMI: 0.968\n\n**Control comparisons**:\n- HVG silhouette: 0.283 (stronger than metabolic features)\n- Random control silhouette range: 0.20 - 0.22\n- Metabolic-matched KMeans silhouette: 0.220\n- Random controls exceeding or equal: 2 of 19 (add-one rank fraction: 0.15)\n\n**Confounder analysis**:\n- Patient NMI: 0.518\n- Sample NMI: 0.548\n- Cell type NMI: 0.496\n- Cell subtype NMI: 0.648\n\n**Conclusion**: Inconclusive. The analysis describes cell-group separation using metabolic genes but does not establish discrete biological metabolic states. The HVG baseline shows stronger clustering than metabolic features. Random controls show similar silhouette values, and the random control exceedance fraction (0.15) is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n\n### Question 2: Cell Type Association\n\n**Global cell-type association**:\n- Cell type global NMI: 0.496\n- Within-replicate NMI range: 0.379 - 0.745 (median: 0.592)\n- Replicates assessable: 6\n\n**Cell subtype association**:\n- Cell subtype global NMI: 0.648\n- Within-replicate NMI range: 0.224 - 0.680 (median: 0.636)\n- Replicates assessable: 6\n\n**Within-replicate sensitivity**:\n- Replicates analyzed: 6\n- Global vs within ARI range: 0.071 - 0.676\n- Weakest replicate: 3 (3,287 cells)\n\n**Interpretation**: Cell type and cell subtype show moderate association with metabolic clusters (NMI ~0.5-0.65). The within-replicate \n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 8052 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nity**: The within-replicate NMI varies substantially, with some replicates showing weaker associations. This limits the ability to claim robust metabolic state separation.\n\n4. **Random control comparison**: The random control exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value. In the global analysis, 2 of 19 random controls exceeded the metabolic features (0.15 add-one rank fraction). In the CD8 analysis, 17 of 19 exceeded (0.9 add-one rank fraction).\n\n## Limitations\n\n- **Descriptive only**: All metrics are descriptive; no significance tests are performed on data-derived cluster labels.\n- **No independent validation**: Within-replicate reclustering is not held-out donor validation.\n- **Confounder sensitivity**: Patient and sample NMI values indicate potential confounding by these factors.\n- **Continuum vs discrete**: The analysis does not test whether metabolic states are discrete or continuous.\n- **Symbol-level aggregation**: Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization, which preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs.\n\n## Files\n\n- `report/main.tex` - LaTeX report\n- `report/main.pdf` - Compiled PDF report\n- `results/summary.json` - Global analysis summary\n- `results/analysis_manifest.json` - Analysis manifest with all metadata\n- `results/core_analysis/core_result.json` - Global core analysis results\n- `results/core_analysis/cluster_labels.csv` - Global cluster labels\n- `results/core_analysis/matched_metabolic_genes.txt` - Matched metabolic genes\n- `results/core_analysis/baseline_metrics.csv` - HVG and random control metrics\n- `results/core_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf` - Global metabolic UMAP\n- `results/core_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf` - Global clustering diagnostics\n- `results/cd8_analysis/core_result.json` - CD8 T cell core analysis results\n- `results/cd8_analysis/cluster_labels.csv` - CD8 T cell cluster labels\n- `results/cd8_analysis/matched_metabolic_genes.txt` - Matched metabolic genes for CD8\n- `results/cd8_analysis/baseline_metrics.csv` - CD8 T cell baseline metrics\n- `results/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf` - CD8 T cell metabolic UMAP\n- `results/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf` - CD8 T cell clustering diagnostics\n- `inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv` - User-supplied metabolic genes\n- `inputs/Exp_data_UMIcounts.mtx` - Expression matrix\n- `inputs/Cells.csv` - Cell metadata\n- `inputs/Genes.txt` - Gene list\n\n## References\n\n1. Choudhury, A., Magill, S. T., Eaton, C. D., et al. (2022). Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities. *Nature Genetics*, 54(5), 649-659. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8\n\n## Verification\n\nThe analysis was performed using the `workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2` engine. Core result hashes:\n- Global core_result.json: 0da19b546073463f6341cb411b21287f3e50b2b9733a6f39dfc53e1fb9ca5848\n- CD8 core_result.json: 50950f343600c9c0dd17ed5191a3511cc2fb397d1b026120f4fe47ca6ab1f102\n"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:85","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fe2d1d3da15ab04","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:47:08.348Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Metabolic State Analysis Using Single-Cell RNA-Seq}\n\\author{Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nThis analysis examines transcriptional metabolic states in single-cell RNA-seq data using the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset (3CA:20773) with user-supplied metabolic genes.\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\\subsection{Data Processing}\nThe analysis uses the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset from the Curated Cancer Cell Atlas (3CA:20773). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes from 10 meningioma samples.\n\nUser-supplied metabolic genes from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv} were used for clustering. The input file contains 1,988 source gene symbols, with 1,646 matched to expression features after exact and case-insensitive matching.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were processed using the following normalization pipeline:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{normalized}_{ij} = \\log_1p\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI}_{ij}}{\\text{library}_i} \\times 10000\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\text{UMI}_{ij}$ is the UMI count for gene $j$ in cell $i$, $\\text{library}_i$ is the total UMI count for cell $i$, and $\\log_1p$ is the natural logarithm of $x+1$.\n\nFeatures were scaled using Scanpy's \\texttt{scale} method with \\texttt{zero\\_center=False} and \\texttt{max\\_value=10}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with the following parameters:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item PCA: 30 components, arpack solver, seed=0\n    \\item Neighbors: 15 neighbors, seed=0\n    \\item Leiden: igraph flavor, 2 iterations, undirected graph\n\\end{itemize}\n\nResolution was selected using a stability filter of \\texttt{mean\\_pairwise\\_ari $\\ge$ 0.75} across multiple seeds, with the primary criterion being highest median silhouette and tie-breaking by highest mean pairwise ARI.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Question 1: Global Metabolic State Clustering}\n\\subsubsection{Analysis Overview}\nUsing all 58,843 cells and 1,646 metabolic genes, the analysis identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cluster size summary for global metabolic state analysis}\n\\label{tab:global_clusters}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nTotal cells analyzed & 58,843 \\\\\nMetabolic genes analyzed & 1,646 \\\\\nNumber of clusters & 17 \\\\\nSelected resolution & 0.8 \\\\\nMinimum cluster size & 714 cells \\\\\nMaximum cluster size & 8,806 cells \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Clustering Metrics}\nThe clustering achieved a silhouette score of 0.217 and a mean pairwise ARI of 0.965 across seeds. The HVG-based control achieved a silhouette of 0.283, while random gene controls (19 controls) had mean silhouette of 0.210.\n\n\\subsubsection{Cell-Type Association}\nThe categorical confounder NMI for cell\\_type is 0.496, a\n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 10235 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\n_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\subsection{Conclusions}\n\\textbf{Question 1:} Using all cells and metabolic genes, the analysis identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8. However, the clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.217) are comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.210), and the confounder NMI for patient (0.518) and sample (0.548) indicates that clustering is partially driven by batch effects. The results are descriptive and do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\textbf{Question 2:} Metabolic clusters show moderate association with cell types (NMI: 0.496) and cell subtypes (NMI: 0.648), but the clustering is not dominated by these factors alone. The patient and sample confounders also contribute significantly.\n\n\\textbf{Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells, 6 clusters were identified at resolution 0.4. However, the clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.099) are lower than the HVG control (0.168) and comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.107). The add-one rank fraction of 0.9 indicates that most random controls performed similarly or better.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette is estimated on a fixed random subset; all QC-passing cells are retained for PCA, graph construction, and clustering.\n    \\item The unsupervised question has no predefined condition contrast; no cell-level differential-expression P values are reported.\n    \\item No significance test is performed on data-derived cluster labels; within-replicate reclustering is descriptive and not held-out donor validation.\n    \\item Random gene controls are matched in size, not expression mean or dispersion; their exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\n    \\item A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\n    \\item Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\n    \\item Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, et al.\n``Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.''\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\n\\bibitem{co_scientist}\nNature.\n``Co-Scientist: A framework for scientific reasoning and meta-review.''\n\\textit{Nature}, 2023.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41586-023-06130-4}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"task_checkpoint:ada8397c4f6f5d545625fcfe84178192e40572b3a63d18c18ef1260ce910ec7d","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a04da7f5d2361309","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:48:52.762Z","result":"Task checkpoint saved. Continue the next concrete action; checkpoint claims are not completion evidence."},{"key":"build_research_report:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a:86","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bde40a47ee38e44a","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T18:49:24.974Z","result":"ERROR: {\"error\": \"pdflatex pass 1 failed with exit code 1:\\nThis is pdfTeX, Version 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.29 (M