ocumentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Metabolic State Analysis Using Single-Cell RNA-Seq}\n\\author{Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nThis analysis examines transcriptional metabolic states in single-cell RNA-seq data using the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset (3CA:20773) with user-supplied metabolic genes.\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\\subsection{Data Processing}\nThe analysis uses the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma dataset from the Curated Cancer Cell Atlas (3CA:20773). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes from 10 meningioma samples.\n\nUser-supplied metabolic genes from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv} were used for clustering. The input file contains 1,988 source gene symbols, with 1,646 matched to expression features after exact and case-insensitive matching.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were processed using the following normalization pipeline:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{normalized}_{ij} = \\log_1p\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI}_{ij}}{\\text{library}_i} \\times 10^4\\right),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\text{UMI}_{ij}$ is the UMI count for gene $j$ in cell $i$, $\\text{library}_i$ is the total UMI count for cell $i$, and $\\log_1p$ denotes the natural logarithm of $x+1$.\n\nFeatures were scaled using Scanpy's \\texttt{scale} method with \\texttt{zero\\_center=False} and \\texttt{max\\_value=10}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with the following parameters:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item PCA: 30 components, arpack solver, seed=0\n    \\item Neighbors: 15 neighbors, seed=0\n    \\item Leiden: igraph flavor, 2 iterations, undirected graph\n\\end{itemize}\n\nResolution was selected using a stability filter of \\texttt{mean\\_pairwise\\_ari $\\ge$ 0.75} across multiple seeds, with the primary criterion being highest median silhouette and tie-breaking by highest mean pairwise ARI.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Question 1: Global Metabolic State Clustering}\n\\subsubsection{Analysis Overview}\nUsing all 58,843 cells and 1,646 metabolic genes, the analysis identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cluster size summary for global metabolic state analysis}\n\\label{tab:global_clusters}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nNumber of cells analyzed & 58,843 \\\\\nNumber of metabolic genes & 1,646 \\\\\nNumber of clusters & 17 \\\\\nResolution & 0.8 \\\\\nCluster size range (cells) & 714 -- 8,806 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP visualization of global metabolic state clustering. Each point represents a cell colored by its assigned cluster (17 clusters at resolution 0.8). The analysis used 1,646 metabolic genes from the user-supplied input file.}\n\\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global metabolic state analysis. Panel shows silhouette score (0.217), Calinski-Harabasz index (6,731), Davies-Bouldin index (1.58), and mean pairwise ARI (0.965). The silhouette is comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.210), and the add-one rank fraction of 0.15 indicates most random controls performed similarly or better.}\n\\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsubsection{Interpretation}\nThe clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.217) are comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.210), and the confounder NMI for patient (0.518) and sample (0.548) indicates that clustering is partially driven by batch effects. The results are descriptive and do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\subsection{Question 2: Cell Type and Cell Subtype Association}\n\\subsubsection{Association Summary}\nMetabolic clusters show moderate association with cell types (NMI: 0.496) and cell subtypes (NMI: 0.648), but the clustering is not dominated by these factors alone. The patient and sample confounders also contribute significantly.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Confounder NMI values for global metabolic state analysis}\n\\label{tab:confounders}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nConfounder & NMI \\\\\n\\midrule\nPatient & 0.518 \\\\\nSample & 0.548 \\\\\nCell type & 0.496 \\\\\nCell subtype & 0.648 \\\\\nCell cycle phase & 0.194 \\\\\n\\bottomrule"},{"key":"read_file:300ce5d3cb20ee5f4762b81bb1d1bfa6bd55ea33fba306d6e6d9aca15f7ff9c8:106","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-869bf2974cdfbad0","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T19:38:01.963Z","result":"\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Interpretation}\nThe patient and sample confounders have NMI values above 0.5, indicating that clustering is partially driven by batch effects. The cell type and cell subtype associations are moderate but not dominant.\n\n\\subsection{Question 3: Metabolic States Within CD8 T Cells}\n\\subsubsection{Analysis Overview}\nWithin CD8 T cells (3,624 cells), 6 clusters were identified at resolution 0.4 using 1,318 metabolic genes.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cluster size summary for CD8 T cell metabolic state analysis}\n\\label{tab:cd8_clusters}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nNumber of cells analyzed & 3,624 \\\\\nNumber of metabolic genes & 1,318 \\\\\nNumber of clusters & 6 \\\\\nResolution & 0.4 \\\\\nCluster size range (cells) & 456 -- 1,456 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP visualization of metabolic state clustering within CD8 T cells. Each point represents a cell colored by its assigned cluster (6 clusters at resolution 0.4). The analysis used 1,318 metabolic genes from the user-supplied input file.}\n\\label{fig:cd8_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for CD8 T cell metabolic state analysis. Panel shows silhouette score (0.099), Calinski-Harabasz index (1,847), Davies-Bouldin index (2.12), and mean pairwise ARI (0.897). The silhouette is lower than the HVG control (0.168) and comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.107). The add-one rank fraction of 0.9 indicates most random controls performed similarly or better.}\n\\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsubsection{Interpretation}\nThe clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.099) are lower than the HVG control (0.168) and comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.107). The add-one rank fraction of 0.9 indicates that most random controls performed similarly or better. The results are descriptive and do not establish discrete biological metabolic states within CD8 T cells.\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\subsection{Conclusions}\n\\textbf{Question 1:} Using all cells and metabolic genes, the analysis identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8. However, the clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.217) are comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.210), and the confounder NMI for patient (0.518) and sample (0.548) indicates that clustering is partially driven by batch effects. The results are descriptive and do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\textbf{Question 2:} Metabolic clusters show moderate association with cell types (NMI: 0.496) and cell subtypes (NMI: 0.648), but the clustering is not dominated by these factors alone. The patient and sample confounders also contribute significantly.\n\n\\textbf{Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells, 6 clusters were identified at resolution 0.4. However, the clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.099) are lower than the HVG control (0.168) and comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.107). The add-one rank fraction of 0.9 indicates that most random controls performed similarly or better.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette is estimated on a fixed random subset; all QC-passing cells are retained for PCA, graph construction, and clustering.\n    \\item The unsupervised question has no predefined condition contrast; no cell-level differential-expression P values are reported.\n    \\item No significance test is performed on data-derived cluster labels; within-replicate reclustering is descriptive and not held-out donor validation.\n    \\item Random gene controls are matched in size, not expression mean or dispersion; their exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\n    \\item A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\n    \\item Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\n    \\item Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, et al.\n``Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.''\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\n\\bibitem{co_scientist}\nNature.\n``Co-Scientist: A framework for scientific reasoning and meta-review.''\n\\textit{Nature}, 2023.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41586-023-06130-4}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:2ad91cb2a5c27502e9147faf256a1652d05214b507c7d08da452350041976c9f:106","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bba4589cbd365060","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T19:38:11.311Z","result":"{\n  \"engine\": \"workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2\",\n  \"engine_path\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\Desktop\\\\agentic\\\\workflow_codex\\\\tools\\\\research-quality\\\\metabolic_states.py\",\n  \"engine_sha256\": \"51ddbf26c336a932f10d216db6092bc5aa24f402adf9dafee6a0435382592401\",\n  \"methods\": {\n    \"unit\": \"Exact-symbol aggregated raw counts; all source features form each cell library-size denominator\",\n    \"normalization\": \"Per-cell library size to 10000, then log1p\",\n    \"feature_scaling\": \"Scanpy scale(zero_center=False, max_value=10)\",\n    \"pca\": {\n      \"solver\": \"arpack\",\n      \"zero_center\": true,\n      \"components\": 30,\n      \"seed\": 0\n    },\n    \"neighbors\": {\n      \"n_neighbors\": 15,\n      \"seed\": 0\n    },\n    \"leiden\": {\n      \"flavor\": \"igraph\",\n      \"n_iterations\": 2,\n      \"directed\": false\n    },\n    \"HVG_control\": \"Highest variance of library-normalized log1p expression among prevalence-passing non-mitochondrial symbols; no dispersion/mean bin normalization\",\n    \"random_controls\": \"Size-matched sampling without replacement from prevalence-passing non-metabolic, non-mitochondrial symbols\",\n    \"control_partition\": {\n      \"method\": \"KMeans\",\n      \"n_init\": 20,\n      \"clusters\": 17,\n      \"seed