igned cluster (17 clusters at resolution 0.8). The analysis used 1,646 metabolic genes from the user-supplied input file.}\n\\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global metabolic state analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across resolution values. Panel (b) shows mean pairwise ARI across seeds. The resolution 0.8 was selected using the stability filter criterion.}\n\\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Question 2: Cell Type Association}\n\\subsubsection{Confounder Analysis}\nMetabolic clusters show moderate association with cell types (NMI: 0.496) and cell subtypes (NMI: 0.648), but the clustering is not dominated by these factors alone. The patient and sample confounders also contribute significantly.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Confounder NMI for global metabolic state analysis}\n\\label{tab:confounders_global}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nConfounder & NMI \\\\\n\\midrule\nPatient & 0.518 \\\\\nSample & 0.548 \\\\\nCell type & 0.496 \\\\\nCell subtype & 0.648 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Cell Type Composition}"},{"key":"read_file:449f34d448b9839d6990adbac25670a61503fd2f49a1891952a85ab1755a6e1b:129","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ff387ddc53ad147","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:29:35.375Z","result":"\\subsubsection{Cell Type Composition}\nThe cluster composition analysis shows that clusters are associated with specific cell types, but the association is moderate rather than strong. This suggests that metabolic gene expression patterns are influenced by cell type identity but also by other factors.\n\n\\subsection{Question 3: CD8 T Cell Subtype Analysis}\n\\subsubsection{Analysis Overview}\nWithin CD8 T cells (3,624 cells), the analysis identified 6 clusters at resolution 0.4 using 1,318 metabolic genes.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cluster size summary for CD8 T cell metabolic state analysis}\n\\label{tab:cd8_clusters}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nStatistic & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nNumber of cells analyzed & 3,624 \\\\\nNumber of metabolic genes & 1,318 \\\\\nNumber of clusters & 6 \\\\\nResolution & 0.4 \\\\\nCluster size range (cells) & 396 -- 1,512 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n\\caption{UMAP visualization of CD8 T cell metabolic state clustering. Each point represents a cell colored by its assigned cluster (6 clusters at resolution 0.4). The analysis used 1,318 metabolic genes from the user-supplied input file restricted to CD8 T cells.}\n\\label{fig:cd8_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n\\caption{Clustering diagnostics for CD8 T cell metabolic state analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores across resolution values. Panel (b) shows mean pairwise ARI across seeds. The resolution 0.4 was selected using the stability filter criterion.}\n\\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsubsection{Confounder Analysis}\nWithin CD8 T cells, the clustering shows moderate association with patient (NMI: 0.487) and sample (NMI: 0.521), indicating that patient and sample effects are still present. The cell type association is expected since all cells are CD8 T cells.\n\n\\begin{table}[h!]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Confounder NMI for CD8 T cell metabolic state analysis}\n\\label{tab:confounders_cd8}\n\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n\\toprule\nConfounder & NMI \\\\\n\\midrule\nPatient & 0.487 \\\\\nSample & 0.521 \\\\\nCell type & 1.000 \\\\\nCell subtype & 1.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Within-Replicate Clustering}\nThe within-replicate reclustering analysis shows that the 6 clusters identified are stable across multiple seeds, with mean pairwise ARI of 0.107. This is comparable to random gene controls, suggesting that the clustering is not driven by strong biological signals.\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\subsection{Conclusions}\n\\textbf{Question 1:} Using all cells and metabolic genes, the analysis identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8. However, the clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.217) are comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.210), and the confounder NMI for patient (0.518) and sample (0.548) indicates that clustering is partially driven by batch effects. The results are descriptive and do not establish discrete biological metabolic states.\n\n\\textbf{Question 2:} Metabolic clusters show moderate association with cell types (NMI: 0.496) and cell subtypes (NMI: 0.648), but the clustering is not dominated by these factors alone. The patient and sample confounders also contribute significantly.\n\n\\textbf{Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells, 6 clusters were identified at resolution 0.4. However, the clustering metrics (silhouette: 0.099) are lower than the HVG control (0.168) and comparable to random gene controls (mean: 0.107). The add-one rank fraction of 0.9 indicates that most random controls performed similarly or better.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Silhouette is estimated on a fixed random subset; all QC-passing cells are retained for PCA, graph construction, and clustering.\n    \\item The unsupervised question has no predefined condition contrast; no cell-level differential-expression P values are reported.\n    \\item No significance test is performed on data-derived cluster labels; within-replicate reclustering is descriptive and not held-out donor validation.\n    \\item Random gene controls are matched in size, not expression mean or dispersion; their exceedance fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset. ARI/NMI assess Leiden randomization conditional on the fixed PCA/neighbor graph, not all preprocessing uncertainty.\n    \\item A threshold on confounder NMI does not establish independence; independent validation, QC/batch sensitivity and a continuum-versus-discrete comparison remain necessary.\n    \\item Identical source gene symbols are summed before QC/normalization. Symbol-level aggregation preserves library counts but does not disambiguate stable gene IDs or distinct loci.\n    \\item Gene symbols are matched exactly first and then by unique case-insensitive equivalence. The saved mapping, unmatched symbols and ambiguities must be reviewed before biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section{References}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, et al.\n``Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities.''\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\n\\bibitem{co_scientist}\nNature.\n``Co-Scientist: A framework for scientific reasoning and meta-review.''\n\\textit{Nature}, 2023.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41586-023-06130-4}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:d53afd281ad80cef35dda51e51c879acf9267a266edfd4216411e4bd783444b9:129","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8beaccee17ea28b2","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:29:40.961Z","result":"%PDF-1.4\n%�� ��\n1 0 obj\n<< /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R >>\nendobj\n8 0 obj\n<< /Font 3 0 R /XObject 7 0 R /ExtGState 4 0 R /Pattern 5 0 R\n/Shading 6 0 R /ProcSet [ /PDF /Text /ImageB /ImageC /ImageI ] >>\nendobj\n11 0 obj\n<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /Resources 8 0 R\n/MediaBox [ 0