7:38.154Z","result":"\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using user-supplied metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within the CD8 T cell subtype.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}"},{"key":"read_file:d7760eb1ee67e8a934fd3a2c3d7aaf3c7d9c00c74d018f945d1af8f2362f88ab:142","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1b7e87d40bfa3c8","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:57:44.601Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Single-Cell Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Metabolic Genes}}\n\\author{QA_R4 Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using user-supplied metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within the CD8 T cell subtype.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nWe analyzed the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The original study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nThe analysis used a user-supplied metabolic gene set from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv}. This file contains 1,988 source gene symbols with mixed-case spelling. The analysis tool performed exact matching followed by case-insensitive matching, resulting in 1,646 matched genes for the global analysis and 1,318 matched genes for the CD8 T cell subset analysis.\n"},{"key":"read_file:4864c15b987c0cbfae4c93065e9dcb674b7e0cf3d82427f0dcd78473f97404f7:142","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4acde67ee93e414","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:57:59.146Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Single-Cell Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Metabolic Genes}}\n\\author{QA_R4 Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using user-supplied metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within the CD8 T cell subtype.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nWe analyzed the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The original study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nThe analysis used a user-supplied metabolic gene set from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv}. This file contains 1,988 source gene symbols with mixed-case spelling. The analysis tool performed exact matching followed by case-insensitive matching, resulting in 1,646 matched genes for the global analysis and 1,318 matched genes for the CD8 T cell subset analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were processed using the standard log-normalization method:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log\\_normalized} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI} + 1}{\\text{library\\_size}}\\right) \\times 10^4\n\\end{equation}\nwhere \\texttt{UMI} is the unique molecular identifier count, \\texttt{library\\_size} is the total UMI count per cell, and the factor $10^4$ scales the expression to a convenient range \\cite{stuart2019}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering and Evaluation}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy with Leiden algorithm. Cluster quality was assessed using silhouette score, Adjusted Rand Index (ARI), and Normalized Mutual Information (NMI) against metadata. Random gene controls (19 draws) were used to establish baseline performance.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\nUsing the user-supplied metabolic genes across all 58,843 cells, we identified 17 clusters. The analysis concluded that the results are \\textbf{inconclusive} for defining discrete metabolic states.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of global metabolic clustering using user-supplied metabolic genes across all 58,843 cells. 17 clusters were identified at the selected resolution. Points are colored by cluster assignment.}"},{"key":"read_file:fffdd79ca80226b5545c93d192af8d7dc2c04b6360f7e1ed20ba04aa8a8c6939:142","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7b2779e50d6ac89","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:58:06.171Z","result":"\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using user-supplied metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within the CD8 T cell subtype.\n\\end{abstract}\n"},{"key":"read_file:c63d0be4b238f04cf533c9557adcf6ebf1d2a7ec74228ba937e760e2f7d5e56b:142","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0001e55948f8d9d","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:58:14.796Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Single-Cell Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Metabolic Genes}}\n\\author{QA_R4 Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using user-supplied metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within the CD8 T cell subtype.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nWe analyzed the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The original study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nThe analysis used a user-supplied metabolic gene set from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv}. This file contains 1,988 source gene symbols with mixed-case spelling. The analysis tool performed exact matching followed by case-insensitive matching, resulting in 1,646 matched genes for the global analysis and 1,318 matched genes for the CD8 T cell subset analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were processed using the standard log-normalization method:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log\\_normalized} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI} + 1}{\\text{library\\_size}}\\right) \\times 10^4\n\\end{equation}\nwhere \\texttt{UMI} is the unique molecular identifier count, \\texttt{library\\_size} is the total UMI count per cell, and the factor $10^4$ scales the expression to a convenient range \\cite{stuart2019}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering and Evaluation}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy with Leiden algorithm. Cluster quality was assessed using silhouette score, Adjusted Rand Index (ARI), and Normalized Mutual Information (NMI) against metadata. Random gene controls (19 draws) were used to establish baseline performance.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\nUsing the user-supplied metabolic genes across all 58,843 cells, we identified 17 clusters. The analysis concluded that the results are \\textbf{inconclusive} for defining discrete metabolic states.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of global metabolic clustering using user-supplied metabolic genes across all 58,843 cells. 17 clusters were identified at the selected resolution. Points are colored by cluster assignment.}\n    \\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n  \n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 8469 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\nMetabolic Groupings Within CD8 T Cells}\n\nWe performed a focused analysis on cells annotated as \\texttt{cell\\_subtype = CD8 T cells} (n = 3,624). This subset analysis identified 6 clusters using 1,318 matched metabolic genes.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of metabolic clustering within CD8 T cells (n = 3,624). 6 clusters were identified at the selected resolution. Points are colored by cluster assignment.}\n    \\label{fig:cd8_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{CD8 T cell subset clustering metrics and resolution selection.}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_metrics}\n    \\begin{tabular}{llr}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Unit} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Cells analyzed & 3,624 & cells \\\\\n        Metabolic genes analyzed & 1,318 & genes \\\\\n        Clusters identified & 6 & \\\\\n        Silhouette score & 0.15 & \\\\\n        Resolution selection criterion & Median silhouette & \\\\\n        Random controls total & 19 & \\\\\n        Random controls exceeding observed & 0 & \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe CD8 T cell subset analysis also concluded that results are \\textbf{inconclusive} for defining discrete metabolic states. The silhouette score (0.15) is weak, and the random control comparison shows no significant exceedance over chance. Within-replicate analysis indicates that clustering is driven by patient/sample structure rather than independent metabolic subtypes.\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\nThis analysis demonstrates that when using user-supplied metabolic genes on meningioma scRNA-seq data, the clustering results are inconclusive for defining discrete metabolic states. The primary drivers of clustering are cell type identity and patient/sample structure, not independent metabolic programs.\n\n\\textbf{Limitations:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Cell-level clustering metrics (silhouette, ARI, NMI) are descriptive and do not constitute statistical significance tests.\n    \\item The analysis does not establish discrete biological metabolic states; it only describes observed separation patterns.\n    \\item Clusters derived from the same expression matrix cannot be used for independent hypothesis testing.\n    \\item Patient and sample confounders strongly influence clustering, limiting biological interpretation.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section{References}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, et al.\n\\textit{Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities}.\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\n\\bibitem{stuart2019}\nAndrew J. Stuart, Tanay K. Butler, Paul Hafemeister, et al.\n\\textit{Comprehensive integration of single-cell data}.\n\\textit{Cell}, 177(7):1888-1902, 2019.\n\\doi{10.1016/j.cell.2019.05.031}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:6eb4c9fa3386b59021259173aae7bc858980d394daccd1a32655e2d1f3dc5167:142","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0fbf559b5d520d2","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:57:53.909Z","result":"\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using user-supplied metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within the CD8 T cell subtype.\n\\end{abstract}\n"},{"key":"write_file:0491da732b3087755d5607ce75fedf57bd840cc3bbae80356e136636fc03c8d0","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-97b45bcae59183d5","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T20:59:50.690Z","result":"Wrote 8471 bytes to report\\main.tex"},{"key":"task_checkpoint:c26ae56e117df674d77760404e2649e23d267b4325019ea11e0c6a93e39536ae","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fd08f4660fb57c9","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T21:00:18.629Z","result":"Task checkpoint saved. Continue the next concrete action; checkpoint claims are not completion evidence."},{"key":"build_research_report:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a:143","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b73aba208047d699","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T21:00:23.699Z","result":"ERROR: {\"error\": \"pdflatex pass 1 failed with exit code 1:\\nThis is pdfTeX, Version 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.29 (MiKTeX 26.5) (preloaded format=pdflatex.fmt)\\n restricted \\\\write18 enabled.\\nentering extended mode\\n(main.tex\\nLaTeX2e <2026-06-01>\\nL3 programming layer <2026-06-18>\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/base\\\\article.cls\\nDocument Class: article 2025/01/22 v1.4n Standard LaTeX document class\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/base\\\\size11.clo))\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/base\\\\inputenc.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKT