n":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T21:43:47.919Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Single-Cell Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Metabolic Genes}}\n\\author{QA\\_R4 Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using user-supplied metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within the CD8 T cell subtype.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nWe analyzed the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The original study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nThe analysis used a user-supplied metabolic gene set from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv}. This file contains 1,988 source gene symbols with mixed-case spelling. The analysis tool performed exact matching followed by case-insensitive matching, resulting in 1,646 matched genes for the global analysis and 1,318 matched genes for the CD8 T cell subset analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were processed using the standard log-normalization method:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log\\_normalized} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI} + 1}{\\text{library\\_size}}\\right) \\times 10^4\n\\end{equation}\nwhere \\texttt{UMI} is the unique molecular identifier count, \\texttt{library\\_size} is the total UMI count per cell, and the factor $10^4$ scales the expression to a convenient range \\cite{stuart2019}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering and Evaluation}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy with Leiden algorithm. Cluster quality was assessed using silhouette score, Adjusted Rand Index (ARI), and Normalized Mutual Information (NMI) against metadata. Random gene controls (19 draws) were used to establish baseline performance.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\nUsing the user-supplied metabolic genes across all 58,843 cells, we identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8. The analysis concluded that results are \\textbf{inconclusive} for defining discrete biological metabolic states.\n\nThe silhouette score (0.217) is positive but weak. The HVG-based silhouette (0.283) exceeds the metabolic gene-based silhouette, indicating that highly variable genes capture more structure than the supplied metabolic genes. The random control comparison shows that 2 of 19 random gene controls exceeded or equal the metabolic gene clustering silhouette, with an add-one rank fraction of 0.15.\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n    \\centering"},{"key":"read_file:de94d7fa88f3a3e62c84c6334443ef3b85ac04cf7975156f89bfe6c4a8100525:159","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b1cd5d2494f891e9","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T21:43:54.292Z","result":"        \\midrule\n        Silhouette score & 0.099 & 0.156 \\\\\n        Mean pairwise ARI & 0.8 & 0.8 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Random controls (n=19) & & \\\\\n        Exceeded or equal & 19 & 0 \\\\\n        Add-one rank fraction & 0.9 & 0.00 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Cell type NMI & 0.987 & 0.999 \\\\\n        Subtype NMI & 0.999 & 1.000 \\\\\n        Patient NMI & 0.393 & 0.951 \\\\\n        Sample NMI & 0.443 & 0.967 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\nThis analysis demonstrates that when using user-supplied metabolic genes on meningioma scRNA-seq data, the clustering results are inconclusive for defining discrete biological metabolic states. The primary drivers of clustering are cell type identity and patient/sample structure, not independent metabolic programs.\n\n\\textbf{Question 1 Answer:} Across all 58,843 cells, metabolic genes reveal 17 clusters at resolution 0.8, but the results are inconclusive for defining discrete biological metabolic states. The silhouette score (0.217) is positive but weak, and the random control comparison shows no significant exceedance over chance (add-one rank fraction = 0.15).\n\n\\textbf{Question 2 Answer:} The metabolic groupings show moderate association with cell type (NMI = 0.496) and stronger association with cell subtype (NMI = 0.648). Clustering is also driven by patient structure (NMI = 0.518). Within-replicate analysis shows that clusters are not consistently reproducible across patients, with global-vs-within ARI ranging from 0.071 to 0.676.\n\n\\textbf{Question 3 Answer:} Within CD8 T cells (n = 3,624), metabolic genes reveal 6 clusters at resolution 0.4, but the results are inconclusive for defining discrete biological metabolic states. The silhouette score (0.099) is weak, and the random control comparison shows no significant exceedance over chance (add-one rank fraction = 0.9). Clustering is driven by patient/sample structure (NMI = 0.393 for patient, 0.443 for sample).\n\n\\textbf{Limitations:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Cell-level clustering metrics (silhouette, ARI, NMI) are descriptive and do not constitute statistical significance tests.\n    \\item The analysis does not establish discrete biological metabolic states; it only describes observed separation patterns.\n    \\item Clusters derived from the same expression matrix cannot be used for independent hypothesis testing.\n    \\item Patient and sample confounders strongly influence clustering, limiting biological interpretation.\n    \\item The random control add-one rank fraction is a descriptive benchmark, not a biological P value.\n    \\item Resolution was selected using the same dataset, limiting generalizability.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section{References}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, et al.\n\\textit{Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities}.\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\doi{10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\n\\bibitem{stuart2019}\nAndrew J. Stuart, Tanay K. Butler, Paul Hafemeister, et al.\n\\textit{Comprehensive integration of single-cell data}.\n\\textit{Cell}, 177(7):1888-1902, 2019.\n\\doi{10.1016/j.cell.2019.05.031}\n\\end{thebibliography}"},{"key":"read_file:92cf93958f07acf37194a9c22a19ad14cbb0060f541012b81c4c126047eae1ad:159","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b786c43026b3fc7","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T21:42:46.206Z","result":"{\n  \"engine\": \"workflow_research_quality_metabolic_states_v2\",\n  \"engine_path\": \"C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\Desktop\\\\agentic\\\\workflow_codex\\\\tools\\\\research-quality\\\\metabolic_states.py\",\n  \"engine_sha256\": \"51ddbf26c336a932f10d216db6092bc5aa24f402adf9dafee6a0435382592401\",\n  \"methods\": {\n    \"unit\": \"Exact-symbol aggregated raw counts; all source features form each cell library-size denominator\",\n    \"normalization\": \"Per-cell library size to 10000, then log1p\",\n    \"feature_scaling\": \"Scanpy scale(zero_center=False, max_value=10)\",\n    \"pca\": {\n      \"solver\": \"