: \"inputs/Genes.txt\",\n    \"genes_sha256\": \"a285759694b2e6fdb495fa4e996643b50e77588013dbba7b1eb3d1926bd95b60\",\n    \"matrix_orientation\": \"genes_by_cells\",\n    \"matrix_shape\": [\n      33538,\n      58843\n    ],\n    \"metabolic_genes_path\": \"inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv\",\n    \"metabolic_genes_sha256\": \"7503180d29693bd1e654ea93281db0e3ec4177ff44842c8b02f6a098402e41a9\",\n    \"gene_set_audit\": {\n      \"format\": \"csv\",\n      \"nonempty_rows\": 1988,\n      \"unique_exact_symbols\": 1988,\n      \"duplicate_exact_rows\": 0,\n      \"casefold_collision_groups\": 0,\n      \"exact_matches\": 341,\n      \"case_insensitive_unique_matches\": 1457,\n      \"unmatched_symbols\": 190,\n      \"ambiguous_casefold_symbols\": 0,\n      \"duplicate_expression_mappings\": 0,\n      \"matched_expression_symbols_before_prevalence\": 1798,\n      \"included_expression_symbols_after_prevalence\": 1318\n    },\n    \"subset\": {\n      \"field\": \"cell_subtype\",\n      \"values\": [\n        \"CD8 T cells\"\n      ],\n      \"source_cells_selected\": 3624\n    }\n  },\n  \"parameters\": {\n    \"seeds\": [\n      0,\n      17,\n      42,\n      73,\n      101\n    ],\n    \"resolutions\": [\n      0.4,\n      0.8,\n      1.2\n    ],\n    \"random_baselines\": 19,\n    \"min_counts\": 500,"},{"key":"read_file:8bc10cc3de0dd5b0e5165ddab63f62a7795ddc09fa48404e226c2ff3413c6ae1:160","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b888fe6d2b93cfcf","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T21:45:48.482Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{margin=1in}\n\n\\title{\\textbf{Single-Cell Metabolic State Analysis Using User-Supplied Metabolic Genes}}\n\\author{QA\\_R4 Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using user-supplied metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within the CD8 T cell subtype.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset}\nWe analyzed the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The original study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nThe analysis used a user-supplied metabolic gene set from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic\\_genes\\_total.csv}. This file contains 1,988 source gene symbols with mixed-case spelling. The analysis tool performed exact matching followed by case-insensitive matching, resulting in 1,646 matched genes for the global analysis and 1,318 matched genes for the CD8 T cell subset analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were processed using the standard log-normalization method:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log\\_normalized} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI} + 1}{\\text{library\\_size}}\\right) \\times 10^4\n\\end{equation}\nwhere \\texttt{UMI} is the unique molecular identifier count, \\texttt{library\\_size} is the total UMI count per cell, and the factor $10^4$ scales the expression to a convenient range \\cite{stuart2019}.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering and Evaluation}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy with Leiden algorithm. Cluster quality was assessed using silhouette score, Adjusted Rand Index (ARI), and Normalized Mutual Information (NMI) against metadata. Random gene controls (19 draws) were used to establish baseline performance.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\nUsing the user-supplied metabolic genes across all 58,843 cells, we identified 17 clusters at resolution 0.8. The analysis concluded that results are \\textbf{inconclusive} for defining discrete biological metabolic states.\n\nThe silhouette score (0.217) is positive but weak. The HVG-based silhouette (0.283) exceeds the metabolic gene-based silhouette, indicating that highly variable genes capture more structure than the supplied metabolic genes. The random control comparison shows that 2 of 19 random gene controls exceeded or equal the metabolic gene clustering silhouette, with an add-one \n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 9175 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Question 3: Metabolic Groupings Within CD8 T Cells}\n\nWithin CD8 T cells (n = 3,624), metabolic genes reveal 6 clusters at resolution 0.4, but the results are inconclusive for defining discrete biological metabolic states. The silhouette score (0.099) is weak, and the random control comparison shows no significant exceedance over chance (add-one rank fraction = 0.9). Clustering is driven by patient/sample structure (NMI = 0.393 for patient, 0.443 for sample).\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n    \\centering\n    \\fbox{\\parbox{0.8\\textwidth}{\\centering UMAP visualization of metabolic gene-based clustering within CD8 T cells (n=3,624). Each point represents a cell colored by its assigned cluster (6 clusters at resolution 0.4).}}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of metabolic gene-based clustering within CD8 T cells (n=3,624). Each point represents a cell colored by its assigned cluster (6 clusters at resolution 0.4).}\n    \\label{fig:cd8_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n    \\caption{Clustering diagnostics for the CD8 T cell subset analysis. Panel (a) shows silhouette scores comparing metabolic genes (0.099) to HVG-based clustering (0.156). Panel (b) displays the distribution of Adjusted Rand Index values between clusters and cell type annotations. Panel (c) shows the cluster size distribution across the 6 clusters.}\n    \\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[htbp]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{CD8 T cell analysis metrics: silhouette scores, random control comparison, and confounder associations. Values are from \\texttt{results/cd8\\_analysis/core\\_result.json}.}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_metrics}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n        \\toprule\n        Metric & Metabolic & HVG \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Silhouette score & 0.099 & 0.156 \\\\\n        Mean pairwise ARI & 0.8 & 0.8 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Random controls (n=19) & & \\\\\n        Exceeded or equal & 19 & 0 \\\\\n        Add-one rank fraction & 0.9 & 0.00 \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Cell type NMI & 0.987 & 0.999 \\\\\n        Subtype NMI & 0.999 & 1.000 \\\\\n        Patient NMI & 0.393 & 0.951 \\\\\n        Sample NMI & 0.443 & 0.967 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\nThis analysis demonstrates that when using user-supplied metabolic genes on meningioma scRNA-seq data, the clustering results are inconclusive for defining discrete biological metabolic states. The primary drivers of clustering are cell type identity and patient/sample structure, not independent metabolic programs.\n\n\\textbf{Question 1 Answer:} Across all 58,843 cells, metabolic genes reveal 17 clusters at resolution 0.8, but the results are inconclusive for defining discrete biological metabolic states. The silhouette score (0.217) is positive but weak, and the random control comparison shows no significant exceedance over chance (add-one rank fraction = 0.15)."},{"key":"re