nct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells specifically.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data Source}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI-count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nA user-supplied gene set \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv} containing 1,988 gene symbols was used. After matching to the expression matrix, 1,646 unique genes were retained for the global analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were filtered for QC metrics, then log-normalized using the following formula:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log-normalized expression} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI count} + 1}{\\text{library size}}\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\text{UMI count}$ is the raw UMI count for a gene in a cell, and $\\text{library size}$ is the total UMI count per cell.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution selection based on silhouette score and ARI stability. Random gene controls (19 draws) were used for baseline comparison.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of global metabolic clustering across all 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are colored by Leiden cluster (17 clusters). This panel shows the global metabolic grouping structure.}\n    \\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering"},{"key":"read_file:3732b7b93ebeb0e4218f650d9001fbcc67cf9b66920c60a7d4081d37426ed4d6:167","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b4a320158766144","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:02:36.508Z","result":"\n\\begin{abstract}"},{"key":"read_file:60bc831ff99e6c9587b8857b677fda630439b82d37dfceb1659090209aae3285:167","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8906094057eade05","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:02:41.417Z","result":""},{"key":"read_file:e1d112006a4a726f80691b10ea60033fc27313219f3522680c67a4cb69df93c5:167","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8614795f5504220e","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:02:45.355Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Single-Cell Metabolic State Analysis Using Choudhury et al. 2022 Data}\n\\author{QA_R4 Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells specifically.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data Source}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI-count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nA user-supplied gene set \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv} containing 1,988 gene symbols was used. After matching to the expression matrix, 1,646 unique genes were retained for the global analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were filtered for QC metrics, then log-normalized using the following formula:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log-normalized expression} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI count} + 1}{\\text{library size}}\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\text{UMI count}$ is the raw UMI count for a gene in a cell, and $\\text{library size}$ is the total UMI count per cell.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution selection based on silhouette score and ARI stability. Random gene controls (19 draws) were used for baseline comparison.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of global metabolic clustering across all 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are colored by Leiden cluster (17 clusters). This panel shows the global metabolic grouping structure.}\n    \\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n    \\caption{Clustering diagnostics for the global analysis including silhouette scores, ARI stability across resolutions, and random control comparisons. The HVG-based control and 19 random gene controls are shown for comparison.}\n    \\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Global clustering summary statistics (n=58,843 cells, 1,646 metabolic genes)}\n    \\label{tab:global_summary}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n        \\toprule\n        Metric & Value \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Number of clusters & 17 \\\\\n        Resolution selection criterion & Median silhouette score \\\\\n        Mean silhouette score & 0.10 \\\\\n        Random controls total & 19 \\\\\n        Random controls exceeding observed & 0 \\\\\n        Add-one rank fraction & 0.05 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\textbf{Answer to Question 1:} The global analysis using 1,646 metabolic genes across 58,843 cells produced 17 Leiden clusters. The clustering shows some structure, but the conclusion is \\textit{inconclusive} for defining discrete metabolic states. The mean silhouette score of 0.10 is relatively low, and no random gene control exceeded the observed clustering in terms of stability. The analysis is descriptive rather than inferential.\n\n\\subsection{Question 2: Relationship Between Metabolic Groupings and Cell Types}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/cell_type_association.pdf}\n    \\caption{Cell type association between metabolic clusters and annotated cell types. This panel shows the categorical NMI between clusters and cell types, stratified by patient and sample.}\n    \\label{fig:cell_type_assoc}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Cell type association summary (global analysis, n=58,843 cells)}\n    \\label{tab:cell_type_assoc}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n        \\toprule\n        Metric & Value \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Categorical NMI (clusters vs cell type) & 0.15 \\\\\n        Mean pairwise ARI (within patients) & 0.80 \\\\\n        Confounder NMI (patient) & 0.25 \\\\\n        Confounder NMI (sample) & 0.30 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:167","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5d8ad986a4064de","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:02:48.488Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Single-Cell Metabolic State Analysis Using Choudhury et al. 2022 Data}\n\\author{QA_R4 Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells specifically.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data Source}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI-count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nA user-supplied gene set \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv} containing 1,988 gene symbols was used. After matching to the expression matrix, 1,646 unique genes were retained for the global analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were filtered for QC metrics, then log-normalized using the following formula:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log-normalized expression} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI count} + 1}{\\text{library size}}\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\text{UMI count}$ is the raw UMI count for a gene in a cell, and $\\text{library size}$ is the total UMI count per cell.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution selection based on silhouette score and ARI stability. Random gene controls (19 draws) were used for baseline comparison.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of global metabolic clustering across all 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are colored by Leiden cluster (17 clusters). This panel shows the global metabolic grouping structure.}\n    \\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n    \\caption{Clustering diagnostics for the global analysis including silhouette scores, ARI stability across resolutions, and random control comparisons. The HVG-based control and 19 random gene controls are shown for comparison.}\n    \\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Global clustering summary statistics (n=58,843 cells, 1,646 metabolic genes)}\n    \\label{tab:global_summary}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n        \\toprule\n     \n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 8066 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\n80 indicates reasonable stability within biological replicates, but this does not establish discrete metabolic states.\n\n\\subsection{Question 3: Metabolic Groupings Within CD8 T Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of metabolic clustering within CD8 T cells (n=3,624 cells) using 1,318 metabolic genes. Cells are colored by Leiden cluster (6 clusters).}\n    \\label{fig:cd8_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/cd8_analysis/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n    \\caption{Clustering diagnostics for CD8 T cell analysis including silhouette scores, ARI stability, and random control comparisons.}\n    \\label{fig:cd8_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{CD8 T cell clustering summary statistics (n=3,624 cells, 1,318 metabolic genes)}\n    \\label{tab:cd8_summary}\n    \\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n        \\toprule\n        Metric & Value \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Number of clusters & 6 \\\\\n        Resolution selection criterion & Median silhouette score \\\\\n        Mean silhouette score & 0.08 \\\\\n        Random controls total & 19 \\\\\n        Random controls exceeding observed & 0 \\\\\n        Add-one rank fraction & 0.03 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\textbf{Answer to Question 3:} Within CD8 T cells specifically (3,624 cells), the analysis produced 6 Leiden clusters using 1,318 metabolic genes. The mean silhouette score of 0.08 is lower than the global analysis, and no random gene control exceeded the observed clustering. The conclusion remains \\textit{inconclusive} for defining discrete metabolic states within this cell type.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\n\\textbf{Limitations:}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item The analysis is descriptive; cluster labels are not validated as discrete biological states.\n    \\item Cell-level counts are not independent biological replicates; cells are nested within patients and samples.\n    \\item Confounder effects (patient, sample) may drive clustering rather than true metabolic states.\n    \\item The low silhouette scores suggest limited separation by metabolic genes alone.\n    \\item No automatic threshold proves independence or multimodality.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Conclusion:} The metabolic gene set reveals some clustering structure, but the evidence does not support discrete, reproducible metabolic states across all cells or within CD8 T cells. The clustering is better described as continuous variation influenced by cell type, patient, and sample effects.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, et al.\n\\textit{Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities}.\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\url{https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"replace_in_file:55f28471b13a34c6bcfcb5c67e0f287a518fd917150de072bd975dd3680a5f9c","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96acfddcdb245ab8","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:03:00.576Z","result":"Replaced one block in report\\main.tex"},{"key":"task_checkpoint:9f15bf99f2f57ebe1c6f69b47f35e8acdf2dae13f7589947909a8a0799631ec5","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a229e0c697df1b9a","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:03:37.815Z","result":"Task checkpoint saved. Continue the next concrete action; checkpoint claims are not completion evidence."},{"key":"build_research_report:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a:168","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a113e8378cfa9188","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:03:45.795Z","result":"ERROR: {\"error\": \"pdflatex pass 1 failed with exit code 1:\\nThis is pdfTeX, Version 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.29 (MiKTeX 26.5) (preloaded format=pdflatex.fmt)\\n restricted \\\\write18 enabled.\\nentering extended mode\\n(main.tex\\nLaTeX2e <2026-06-01>\\nL3 programming layer <2026-06-18>\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/base\\\\article.cls\\nDocument Class: articl