ds\\\\pdftexcmds.sty\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/generic/infwarerr\\\\infwarerr.sty)))\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/hycolor\\\\hycolor.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/hyperref\\\\nameref.sty\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/refcount\\\\refcount.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/generic/gettitlestring\\\\gettitlestring\\n.sty (C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/kvoptions\\\\kvoptions.sty)))\\n (C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/etoolbox\\\\etoolbox.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/generic/stringenc\\\\stringenc.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/hyperref\\\\pd1enc.def)\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/generic/intcalc\\\\intcalc.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/hyperref\\\\puenc.def)\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/url\\\\url.sty)\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/generic/bitset\\\\bitset.sty\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/generic/bigintcalc\\\\bigintcalc.sty)))\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/hyperref\\\\hpdftex.def\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/rerunfilecheck\\\\rerunfilecheck.s\\nty\\n(C:\\\\Users\\\\User\\\\AppData\\\\Roaming\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/generic/uniquecounter\\\\uniquecounter.s\\nty))) (C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/l3backend\\\\l3backend-pdftex.def)\\n(main.aux) (C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/context/base/mkii\\\\supp-pdf.mkii\\n[Loading MPS to PDF converter (version 2006.09.02).]\\n) (C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/epstopdf-pkg\\\\epstopdf-base.sty\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/00miktex\\\\epstopdf-sys.cfg)) (main.out)\\n(main.out)\\nmain.tex:15: Missing $ inserted.\\n<inserted text> \\n                $\\nl.15 \\n     \\nmain.tex:15:  ==> Fatal error occurred, no output PDF file produced!\\nTranscript written on main.log.\\n\\npdflatex: major issue: So far, you have not checked for updates as a MiKTeX user.\"}"},{"key":"read_file:1556ea8626727086185f2c08ff8354e8b29aaa81f11b0bb9f3cc293764bbe3b6:176","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9237e0d1621fa8da","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:36:15.216Z","result":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\n\\title{Single-Cell Metabolic State Analysis Using Choudhury et al. 2022 Data}\n\\author{QA_R4 Research Analysis}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells specifically.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data Source}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI-count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8) \\cite{choudhury2022}.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nA user-supplied gene set \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv} containing 1,988 gene symbols was used. After matching to the expression matrix, 1,646 unique genes were retained for the global analysis.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization}\nCells were filtered for QC metrics, then log-normalized using the following formula:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log-normalized expression} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI count} + 1}{\\text{library size}}\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\text{UMI count}$ is the raw UMI count for a gene in a cell, and $\\text{library size}$ is the total UMI count per cell.\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Scanpy's Leiden algorithm with resolution selection based on silhouette score and ARI stability. Random gene controls (19 draws) were used for baseline comparison.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\n\\subsection{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/metabolic_umap.pdf}\n    \\caption{UMAP visualization of global metabolic clustering across all 58,843 cells using 1,646 metabolic genes. Cells are colored by Leiden cluster (17 clusters). This panel shows the global metabolic grouping structure.}\n    \\label{fig:global_umap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\textwidth]{figures/clustering_diagnostics.pdf}\n    \\caption{Clustering diagnostics for global metabolic analysis including silhouette scores, ARI stability, and random control comparisons. The panel shows the resolution selection filter and median silhouette criterion.}\n    \\label{fig:global_diagnostics}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\caption{Global metabolic clustering metrics for all cells}\n    \\label{tab:global_metrics}\n    \\begin{tabular}{llr}\n        \\toprule\n        Metric & Value & Unit \\\\\n       \n...[OUTPUT TRUNCATED: kept first 3000 and last 3000 of 21673 characters; use filters or save output to a workspace file]...\n The mean silhouette score of 0.12 indicates moderate cluster separation, while the mean pairwise ARI of 0.78 suggests reasonable cluster stability.\n\nRandom gene control comparisons (19 draws) showed that 0 random controls exceeded the observed clustering performance, with an add-one rank fraction of 0.04. This suggests the observed clustering is not purely due to random chance.\n\nStratified analysis by patient within CD8 T cells showed varying degrees of association. The categorical confounder NMI for patient was 0.18, and for sample was 0.22, indicating that patient and sample effects are present but not dominant.\n\nWithin-replicate analysis showed an ARI range of 0.62-0.78, with the weakest replicate showing an ARI of 0.62. This indicates moderate reproducibility of metabolic groupings within CD8 T cells across patients.\n\nCluster composition analysis shows that each cluster contains cells from multiple patients, with no single cluster being exclusively composed of cells from one patient. This suggests that metabolic states within CD8 T cells are not purely patient-specific.\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\nThis analysis used a user-supplied set of metabolic genes to explore transcriptional metabolic groupings in single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study. The global analysis identified 17 metabolic clusters across 58,843 cells, with moderate cluster separation (silhouette score = 0.1) and reasonable stability (mean pairwise ARI = 0.8).\n\nThe relationship between metabolic groupings and cell types was assessed, showing that cell type is a significant predictor of metabolic cluster assignment (categorical NMI = 0.45). However, metabolic clusters also contain cells from multiple cell types, suggesting that metabolic states are not perfectly aligned with cell type boundaries.\n\nWithin CD8 T cells specifically, 6 metabolic clusters were identified from 3,624 cells. The clustering showed moderate separation and stability, with patient and sample effects present but not dominant. The within-replicate ARI range of 0.62-0.78 indicates moderate reproducibility of metabolic groupings within CD8 T cells across patients.\n\n\\textbf{Limitations:} This analysis is exploratory and should not be interpreted as definitive identification of discrete biological metabolic states. The clustering results are descriptive and should be validated in independent datasets. The use of a user-supplied gene set introduces potential biases, and the analysis does not account for all possible confounding factors. Cell-level metrics should not be interpreted as independent biological replicates.\n\n\\section{References}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\\bibitem{choudhury2022}\nAbrar Choudhury, Stephen T. Magill, Charlotte D. Eaton, et al.\n\\textit{Meningioma DNA methylation groups identify biological drivers and therapeutic vulnerabilities}.\n\\textit{Nature Genetics}, 54(5):649-659, 2022.\n\\url{https://doi.org/10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8}\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},{"key":"read_file:a68070ca01708bb8e9a5455bcfb81713e51e28fd2bf2a8c0100a9868900f81b3:176","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84cc118cdfca6dec","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T22:36:27.883Z","result":"\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, (2) the relationship between these groupings and known cell types/subtypes, and (3) whether distinct metabolic groupings exist within CD8 T cells specifically.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data and Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data Source}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI-count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 study (3CA:20773), a 10x Genomics scRNA-seq dataset of 58,843 cells from 10 meningioma samples. The study was published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 