een metabolic clusters and cell types, and (3) whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Methods}"},{"key":"read_file:97318fa56d6101a41c89dae0f044a1964d56a6505eec98573c16fe877cb9f5bb:187","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b41cd9358001b923","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-13T23:01:30.924Z","result":"\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 Meningioma study (3CA:20773) using a user-supplied set of 1,988 metabolic genes. We address three questions: (1) whether global metabolic grouping is reproducible across all cells, (2) the relationship between metabolic clusters and cell types, and (3) whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Methods}\n\n\\subsection{Data Processing}\nRaw UMI counts from the Choudhury et al. 2022 Brain study were processed using the user-supplied metabolic gene set. Library size normalization followed the standard log-normalization method:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log-normalized expression} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI counts}}{\\text{library size}} + 1\\right)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere UMI counts are the raw molecular counts, library size is the total UMI count per cell, and the $+1$ prevents log(0).\n\n\\subsection{Clustering}\nClustering was performed using Leiden algorithm with resolution selection based on silhouette score optimization. Random gene controls (19 total) were used for baseline comparison."},{"key":"read_file:7ef29cb93d1d3a79866e6631fd9e837ff446f9f824504959aca93c42101e7087:187","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b61164f5c1760a78","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":