lyzes metabolic state clustering using single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773). We evaluate whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, their association with cell types/subtypes, and whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Data Sources and Processing}\n\n\\subsection{Dataset Overview}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI-count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773), published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes from 10 meningioma samples.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nUser-supplied metabolic genes from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv} were used for clustering. The file contains 1,988 source gene symbols, with 1,646 matched to expression features after case-insensitive deduplication and exact duplicate symbol summation in raw counts.\n"},{"key":"read_file:5199f79320923fc170556f6071f523fd86de00119c287dda52ed9201b732207a:209","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94c14d0d991cd137","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-14T00:08:05.516Z","result":"\\subsection{Dataset Overview}\nThe analysis uses raw UMI-count data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773), published in \\textit{Nature Genetics} (DOI: 10.1038/s41588-022-01061-8). The dataset contains 58,843 cells and 33,538 genes from 10 meningioma samples.\n\n\\subsection{Metabolic Gene Set}\nUser-supplied metabolic genes from \\texttt{inputs/metabolic_genes_total.csv} were used for clustering. The file contains 1,988 source gene symbols, with 1,646 matched to expression features after case-insensitive deduplication and exact duplicate symbol summation in raw counts.\n\n\\subsection{Normalization Method}\nCells were processed using standard 10x Genomics pipeline with log-normalization:\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\text{log\\_normalized} = \\log_2\\left(\\frac{\\text{UMI} + 1}{\\text{library\\_size\\_median}}\\right),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\text{UMI}$ is the unique molecular identifier count, and $\\text{library\\_size\\_median}$ is the median library size across cells.\n\n\\section{Question 1: Reproducible Metabolic Groupings Across All Cells}\n\n\\subsection{Analysis Overview}"},{"key":"read_file:e5ac4d1c132ff790c8687c2808ee8756843019d5728bc4b95507d13434c5eba6:209","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a919274e0eed1ee8","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-14T00:08:09.176Z","result":"\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis report analyzes metabolic state clustering using single-cell RNA-seq data from the Choudhury et al. 2022 meningioma study (3CA:20773). We evaluate whether metabolic genes reveal reproducible transcriptional metabolic groupings across all cells, their association with cell types/subtypes, and whether distinct metabolic states exist within CD8 T cells.\n\\end{abstract}\n"},{"key":"build_research_report:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a:210","call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b775e8a5440d94af","session":"C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\agentic\\workflow_codex\\.runtime\\codex-home\\sessions\\2026\\09\\13\\rollout-2026-09-13T23-08-39-0b785373-9b3.jsonl","timestamp":"2026-09-14T00:09:21.567Z","result":"ERROR: {\"error\": \"pdflatex pass 1 failed with exit code 1:\\nThis is pdfTeX, Version 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.29 (MiKTeX 26.5) (preloaded format=pdflatex.fmt)\\n restricted \\\\write18 enabled.\\nentering extended mode\\n(main.tex\\nLaTeX2e <2026-06-01>\\nL3 programming layer <2026-06-18>\\n(C:\\\\Program Files\\\\MiKTeX\\\\tex/latex/base\\\\article.cls\\nDocument Class: article 2025/01/22 v1.4n Standard LaTeX document class\\n(C